window.ANIMUX_SEARCH=[{"t": "Animux Guide", "s": "Everything you need to make animation on Animux, from your first boot to a finished film.", "u": "index.html", "x": "Animator freedom meets imagination Animux is a free computer system for animators. Start it from a USB stick or in a virtual machine and you get one desktop with about a hundred ready programs for 3D, 2D, stop motion, video, sound and render farms. You install nothing and pay nothing. This guide assumes you have never used Linux . Every step says what to click, and shows a screenshot where it helps. Start here Try it in VirtualBox Make your first render The Animux desktop. The Animux button at the top left opens the menu with all programs, and the dock at the bottom starts the file manager, terminal and web browser. New here? Follow these five steps What is Animux? : what you get and what you need. Try it safely in VirtualBox , or make a USB stick to start a real computer from it. Take the desktop tour : the menu, files and settings. Run the self-test to check that everything works on your computer. Make your first 3D render with Blender, then continue with the other tutorials. When you like it, install Animux on the hard disk so your work and settings are kept. What do you want to make? 3D animation Blender 5.2 with Cycles and EEVEE, plus characters from MakeHuman. Hand-drawn 2D Pencil2D, Krita and TupiTube for frame-by-frame drawing. Cut-out and vector 2D Synfig Studio: automatic in-betweens and bone rigs. Stop motion Clay, LEGO or paper, shot with a webcam. Edit the film Kdenlive: shots, music, titles, export to MP4. Sound and music Audacity, Ardour, LMMS and more. Rendering Cycles, POV-Ray, LuxCore, Mitsuba, appleseed, pbrt. Render farm Many computers rendering one film with Flamenco. Tutorials Your first 3D render In Blender, replace the default cube with a monkey head, give it shiny red paint, add a floor, light it and render a picture. About 30 minutes, no experience needed. A turntable animation Make Suzanne spin once around in four seconds with two keyframes, then render the animation straight to an MP4 video. About 20 minutes. A character with MakeHuman Create a human character with MPFB inside Blender, give it a skeleton, pose it and set a keyframe, or design it in MakeHuman II and bring it over. About 30 minutes. A bouncing ball in Synfig The classic first animation exercise. Make a ball fall, squash and bounce with waypoints and ease in and out, then export a GIF. About 30 minutes. Hand-drawn animation in Pencil2D Draw a short flip-book animation frame by frame with onion skin, play it back and export it as GIF or MP4. About 30 minutes, perfect for beginners and children. Paint an animation in Krita Use Krita's animation workspace to paint a short loop, such as a flickering candle flame, with onion skins, then render it to a video. About 40 minutes. Stop motion with a webcam Set up a tiny studio, capture a clay or LEGO animation with Stopmotion's onion skin, and turn it into a video with sound. Edit your film in Kdenlive Put rendered shots, a title and music together on a timeline and export an MP4 ready for YouTube. Uses the turntable video from the earlier tutorial. About 30 minutes. Command-line rendering and batch jobs Render Blender, Synfig and POV-Ray without opening them, keep renders running after you log out, render overnight, join frames into a video and convert hundreds of images at once. From idea to finished short film The stages of an animated film production and which Animux program to use at each one, with a realistic plan for a first 30-second film. Latest news 2026-10-30 — Animux Genesis64 r16.3 : OpenToonz 1.8, the studio 2D animation program, and the Maya Workflow Kit for Blender. 2026-10-29 — Animux Genesis64 r16.2 : Claude Code in one step from the menu, and a boot menu without syntax errors. 2026-10-27 — Introducing the Animux Guide : A complete, illustrated guide for animators who have never used Linux, installed on every Animux system and readable by AI agents. 2026-10-26 — Animux Genesis64 r16 is here : Blender 5.2 LTS, Gaffer, five new renderers, MakeHuman II, a signed Animux package rep", "sec": ""}, {"t": "What is Animux?", "s": "A free, complete studio for animators on one disc image. What is inside, what you need and how you can use it.", "u": "start/what-is-animux.html", "x": "Animux is an operating system , like Windows or macOS. It replaces or sits beside them and comes with the programs an animation studio needs. It is built on Debian 12 , one of the oldest and most stable Linux systems, and uses the Xfce desktop, which is simple and light. The current release is Animux Genesis64 r16.3 . It comes as one file, animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso (12 GB). The _28 is the build number, not the release. What you get For Main programs 3D modelling and animation Blender 5.2 , MakeHuman II and MPFB , FreeCAD, Wings 3D, MeshLab Rendering Cycles and EEVEE in Blender, POV-Ray, LuxCoreRender, Mitsuba, appleseed, pbrt 2D animation Synfig Studio , Pencil2D and TupiTube , Krita Stop motion Stopmotion Drawing and images Krita , GIMP, Inkscape, MyPaint Video Kdenlive , OpenShot, Cinelerra-GG, Avidemux, HandBrake, FFmpeg, DJV Sound and music Audacity, Ardour, LMMS, Hydrogen, MuseScore Studio pipeline Gaffer , OpenUSD, Alembic, OpenEXR, OpenColorIO Render farm Flamenco AI (offline) Upscayl (image upscaling), Whisper, rembg, Ollama, ComfyUI, LTX (subtitles, background removal, chat models, Stable Diffusion, video API), AI agents in Blender, GIMP, Krita and Inkscape (MCP) The software list shows all of them. Also included: a web browser (Firefox), office-style viewers, a self-test that checks every renderer, sample scenes, and this guide. Three ways to use Animux In a virtual machine : a pretend computer inside Windows, macOS or Linux. Nothing on your computer changes. It is the best way to look around. → VirtualBox guide Live from a USB stick : start a real computer from the stick. It runs at full speed, and the hard disk is not touched. Anything you save inside the live system is lost when you switch off , unless you save it to a USB drive. → USB stick guide Installed on a disk : Animux becomes a normal system, and your files and settings stay. → Installation guide Not sure? Start with VirtualBox. A virtual machine is slower than a real computer, especially for 3D, but it is completely safe. When you like Animux, put it on a USB stick or install it. What your computer needs Minimum Recommended Processor 64-bit Intel or AMD, 2 cores 4 or more cores, made in 2013 or later Memory (RAM) 4 GB 16 GB or more Disk space (to install) 40 GB 100 GB or more USB stick (live use) 16 GB 32 GB, USB 3 Graphics any a recent AMD, Intel or NVIDIA card Very old processors Blender 5, appleseed, Mitsuba and LuxCoreRender need a processor with SSE4.2 ; Mitsuba and LuxCore also need AVX . Almost every Intel or AMD processor sold since about 2011 has both. On an older one these programs do not start; the self-test tells you so. See Requirements for how to check. Is it really free? Yes. Animux and almost all its programs are free and open-source software : you may use them for any purpose, including paid commercial work, copy them and share them. The work you make belongs to you. Each program has its own licence; see Licences and credits . Words you will meet ISO file : one file that contains a whole disc. You use it to make a USB stick or to start a virtual machine. Live session : Animux running from the USB stick or ISO, without being installed. Render : let the computer calculate the final picture or film from a 3D scene. Terminal : a window where you type commands. You rarely need it; this guide gives every command in full. More in the Glossary .", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Animux at a glance", "s": "All programs on one screen, in the order of an animation production, on the Debian 12 base.", "u": "start/at-a-glance.html", "x": "83 tested programs One animation pipeline, from the first sketch to the finished film. 1 Drawing and storyboards 9 Krita 5.3 GIMP 3.2 Inkscape 1.4 MyPaint 2.0 GrafX2 2.8 mtPaint 3.40 ImageMagick and G'MIC Scribus 1.5 PDF Arranger and Document Viewer 2 2D animation 5 OpenToonz Synfig Studio 1.5 Pencil2D 0.6 TupiTube Stopmotion 0.8 3 3D animation 9 Blender 5.2 MPFB 2.0 Maya Workflow Kit for Blender MakeHuman II 2.0 FreeCAD 0.20 OpenSCAD 2021.01 Wings 3D 2.2 MeshLab 2020.09 Geomview 1.9 4 Lighting and rendering 6 Cycles and EEVEE POV-Ray 3.7 appleseed LuxCoreRender Mitsuba 3 3.9 pbrt-v4 5 Pipeline formats 8 Gaffer 1.7 OpenUSD tools Alembic tools Open Shading Language OpenEXR and OpenImageIO tools OpenColorIO tools OpenVDB tools 10.0 Animux VFX Python 2026.10 6 Render farm 2 Flamenco 3.9 Farmerjoe 7 Sound 9 Ardour 7.3 Audacity 3.2 LMMS 1.2 Hydrogen MuseScore 3 3.2 Rosegarden 22.12 Synthesizers Sound analysis and conversion Audio setup 8 Edit and delivery 13 Kdenlive 22.12 OpenShot 2.6 Cinelerra-GG Avidemux 2.8 HandBrake 1.6 FFmpeg 5.1 MKVToolNix 74.0 Subtitle Editor and Gnome Subtitles DJV 3.7 VLC 3.0 Ristretto and Eye of GNOME K3b 22.12 Timeshift 22.11 9 AI assistants 9 Upscayl 2.15 Whisper rembg 2.0 Ollama ComfyUI MCP servers LTX LTX-2 local Python for AI and data 10 System and tools 13 Thunar File Manager 4.18 Catfish and Disk Usage Analyzer Firefox ESR Xfce Terminal Screenshot Synaptic Package Manager 0.91 GParted and Disks Virtual machines and containers Flatpak 1.14 Animux Self-Test 1.0 Animux Sample Scenes 1.0 Animux Guide 1.0 Install DaVinci Resolve Debian 12 “bookworm” The stable base: Debian 12 bookworm, Linux 6.1, Xfce 4.18. Debian supports each release for years (Debian 12 with security updates until June 2028), and Animux adds current creative software from its own repository. Point at a program to see what it does; a click opens its page where there is one. The software list has every program with its command, and Versions has every version number.", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Used in film production", "s": "Films and studios whose storyboards, textures, frame fixes, colour and 3D were made with programs that Animux ships, with the sources.", "u": "start/in-production.html", "x": "2012 · Blender Foundation open movie Tears of Steel Storyboards drawn in Krita The storyboards were drawn digitally in Krita by David Revoy, the film's concept artist. He described the workflow in his making-of article Tears-of-Steel making-of: storyboard workflow (28 February 2012). The film itself was made with Blender and is released under the Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 licence, so anyone may study it. Source: David Revoy, davidrevoy.com/article113/digital-open-source-storyboard-workflow 1999 and 2002 · Rhythm & Hues · Sony Pictures Imageworks Stuart Little and Stuart Little 2 Textures and frame fixes in Film Gimp, a version of GIMP Film Gimp was a branch of GIMP for film work (later renamed CinePaint). At Rhythm & Hues it painted textures, made paint fixes to rendered frames, removed dust, rigs and wires, and painted mattes. Stuart Little is among the films it was used on. Sony Pictures Imageworks continued with Film Gimp on Stuart Little 2 . Source: Film Gimp project history, filmgimp.sourceforge.net/docs/history.html Since 1995 · Studio Ghibli Studio Ghibli films Ink and paint, colour and compositing in Toonz, today OpenToonz Studio Ghibli chose Toonz during Princess Mononoke (1995). Its own version, the Toonz Ghibli Edition, has been used for ink and paint, colour design and digital composition on most Ghibli titles since The Secret World of Arrietty (2010). In 2016 Dwango released that software as open source: OpenToonz, which Animux builds and ships. Source: Animation World Network, \"Open-Source Animation Production Software 'OpenToonz' Available March 26\", 18 March 2016 2024 · Dream Well Studio, Latvia Flow Made in Blender , rendered in EEVEE Gints Zilbalodis's film about a cat in a flooded world was made by a small team entirely in Blender and rendered with EEVEE, Blender's real-time render engine. It won the Academy Award for Best Animated Feature at the 97th Academy Awards on 2 March 2025, ahead of films from Pixar, DreamWorks and Aardman. The director thanked Blender in his acceptance. Source: CDM, \"How Latvia's Flow used open source Blender in Oscar animation win\", March 2025 Since 2019 · Ubisoft Animation Studio Ubisoft Animation Studio Main 3D tool: Blender In July 2019 Ubisoft Animation Studio, the animation division of Ubisoft Film & Television, adopted Blender as its main digital content creation tool, replacing its in-house software, and joined the Blender Development Fund as a gold member. It also released Mixer, its tool for several artists working in one Blender scene at once, as open source. Source: Ubisoft News, \"Ubisoft Joins Blender Development Fund to Support Open Source Animation\", 22 July 2019 Animux ships the current versions of these programs: Krita for storyboards and paintings, GIMP 3 for plates, textures and frame fixes, OpenToonz for 2D animation, Blender for 3D. See Animux at a glance for all programs.", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Computer requirements", "s": "Check your processor, memory, disk and graphics card before you start, and learn what each one affects.", "u": "start/requirements.html", "x": "Quick check Part Minimum Good What it affects Processor (CPU) 64-bit, 2 cores, SSE4.2 4–16 cores, AVX2 Render speed, simulations, video export Memory (RAM) 4 GB 16–32 GB How big your scenes can be. The live session also keeps its files in RAM. Disk 40 GB free 100 GB+ on an SSD Installing, project files, renders Graphics card (GPU) any that shows a picture AMD Radeon or NVIDIA GeForce from the last 6 years Smooth 3D views in Blender, Krita canvas speed, Upscayl Screen 1280 × 720 1920 × 1080 or larger Blender and video editors need room Network not needed wired or Wi-Fi Updates, render farm Does my processor have SSE4.2 and AVX? Animux itself runs on any 64-bit PC. Four programs need newer processor features: Program Needs Blender 5.2 SSE4.2 appleseed SSE4.2 Mitsuba 3 SSE4.2 and AVX LuxCoreRender SSE4.2 and AVX Intel Core processors from 2011 onwards (\"2nd generation\" Core i3/i5/i7, model numbers 2xxx and higher) and AMD processors from 2011 onwards (Bulldozer, Ryzen and all later ones) have both. On Windows , find your processor name: press Windows key + Pause , or open Settings → System → About . Search the web for the name plus \"AVX\". On Animux (live or installed), open the terminal and type: grep -o -w -E 'sse4_2|avx|avx2' /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u If you see sse4_2 and avx , everything works. Virtual machines A virtual machine shows the guest the host's processor features, except in some setups. QEMU uses a basic processor model unless you add -cpu host , and VirtualBox shows the guest the real processor. If Blender says \"illegal instruction\" in a VM, see Troubleshooting . Graphics cards Intel and AMD graphics work out of the box with the free drivers included in Animux. NVIDIA cards work with the free nouveau driver included in Animux. It is fine for the desktop but slow for 3D. The official NVIDIA driver is not on the ISO; on an installed system you can add it ( Adding software ). GPU rendering (Cycles on the graphics card) needs the official NVIDIA driver (CUDA/OptiX) or AMD's ROCm (HIP). Without them, Cycles renders on the processor, which always works. Memory and the live session In a live session, everything you create (and every program update) lives in memory until you switch off. With 4 GB of RAM you can work, but keep projects small, and save to a USB drive often. With 8 GB or more it is comfortable. Drawing tablets Wacom, Huion, XP-Pen and other tablets are recognised as pen devices with pressure in Krita, GIMP, MyPaint and Blender. For tablet button setup you can install the xserver-xorg-input-wacom package on an installed system.", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Get the ISO file", "s": "Download Animux, check that the download is complete and undamaged, and know which file is the newest.", "u": "start/get-the-iso.html", "x": "Animux comes as one ISO file , a copy of a whole disc in a single file. For release r16.3 it is: File Size Release animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso about 12 GB Genesis64 r16.3 The number after deb_ counts the builds, not the releases. Always use the newest release named on the News page. Download Download the ISO from the Animux download page, or copy it from someone who already has it (a USB disk works fine; it is free to share). The file is large, so: use a wired connection or a good Wi-Fi signal; make sure the disk you save it to has at least 15 GB free ; on a USB disk formatted as FAT32 , files over 4 GB do not fit. Use exFAT or NTFS . Check the download (recommended) A broken download causes strange errors later. Compare the file's SHA-256 checksum with the one published next to the download (a long string of 64 letters and digits). Windows – open PowerShell (Start menu → type powershell ) and type: Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 \"$HOME\\Downloads\\animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso\" macOS – open Terminal : shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso Linux (including Animux): sha256sum ~/Downloads/animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso The long string printed must match the published one exactly. If it does not, download again. Next step Try it in a virtual machine: VirtualBox or other virtual machines . Start a real computer from it: Make a USB stick .", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Try Animux in VirtualBox", "s": "Create a virtual machine with its own 80 GB virtual disk, start Animux from the ISO file and, if you like, install it, all without touching your real computer.", "u": "start/virtualbox.html", "x": "A virtual machine (VM) is a pretend computer that runs in a window on your real computer. Animux inside it cannot see or change your files. VirtualBox is a free VM program for Windows, macOS (Intel) and Linux. You need: the Animux ISO file ( animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso , 12 GB). See Get the ISO . VirtualBox 7 from https://www.virtualbox.org/wiki/Downloads (\"Windows hosts\", \"macOS / Intel hosts\" or your Linux system). about 100 GB free disk space on the real computer (the ISO plus a virtual disk that grows up to 80 GB), and at least 8 GB RAM . Fast way: let a script do steps 2 to 4 On Windows you can download create-animux-vm.ps1 , right-click it and choose Run with PowerShell . It asks for the ISO file and creates the VM with exactly the settings below. Then continue at step 5 . 1. Install VirtualBox Run the VirtualBox installer and accept the defaults. On Windows it may warn that the network will be briefly disconnected; that is normal. Start Oracle VirtualBox when it is done. 2. Create the virtual machine Click New (the blue star). Fill in: Field Value Name Animux Folder where the VM files go. Pick a disk with 100 GB free (for example D:\\VMs ). ISO Image select animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso Type / Subtype / Version Linux / Debian / Debian (64-bit) Unattended installation off . In VirtualBox 7.1 and later leave Proceed with Unattended Installation unticked; in 7.0 tick Skip Unattended Installation . Animux has its own installer, and VirtualBox's automatic installation does not work with it. Click Next . 3. Memory, processors and firmware Setting Value Why Base Memory 8192 MB (8 GB); 4096 MB is the minimum Programs and the live session live in memory Processors 4 (or half of your real cores) Rendering uses every core Enable EFI leave off Animux starts with both, but \"off\" (BIOS) is the setting tested most. Tick it only if you want to test UEFI. Click Next . 4. The virtual hard disk (storage) Choose Create a Virtual Hard Disk Now and set the size to 80 GB . Leave Pre-allocate Full Size unticked: the file then starts small and grows only as Animux uses it. Do I need the disk to try Animux? No. The live session runs without one. You need the disk if you want to install Animux inside the VM so your work is kept. Click Next , then Finish . Recommended settings Select the new VM and click Settings : Page Setting Value Display → Screen Video Memory 128 MB Graphics Controller VMSVGA Enable 3D Acceleration off System → Motherboard Boot Order Optical first, then Hard Disk Network → Adapter 1 Attached to NAT (the default; gives internet access) Audio Enable Audio on (default) Click OK . 5. Start Animux Select the VM and click Start . After a few seconds you see the Animux boot menu: The Animux boot menu. Press Enter on the first entry, \"Animator Workstation\". If you do nothing, it starts by itself after a few seconds. Press Enter on Animux Genesis64 – Animator Workstation . Some text scrolls past for a moment, then the desktop opens by itself (about a minute in a VM). You are logged in as the live user user (password user ). Animux running in VirtualBox (1024 × 768 screen). To learn what each boot menu entry does, see The boot menu . Using the mouse and keyboard in the VM Click inside the window to use Animux. If the mouse gets \"caught\", press the Host key (right Ctrl on Windows and Linux, left ⌘ Command on Mac) to release it. 6. Check that everything works Open the menu at the top left: Animux → Animux Tools → Animux Self-Test . After a few minutes it lists every test: The self-test in VirtualBox: 29 tests passed. See The self-test for what the results mean. 7. Install Animux on the virtual disk (optional) In the live session, everything you do is lost when the VM is switched off. To keep your work, install Animux on the 80 GB virtual disk: Restart the VM. In the boot menu choose Install Animux Genesis64 to Disk . Follow the installation guide . Choose Erase disk : inside the VM this erases only the empty virtual disk, never your real one", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Other virtual machines", "s": "Settings for QEMU and virt-manager, VMware, Hyper-V and Parallels, and the one setting that matters most for 3D programs.", "u": "start/other-vms.html", "x": "Any virtual machine program that can start a 64-bit Linux ISO can run Animux. Use the same sizes everywhere: Setting Value Guest type Linux, Debian 12 64-bit Memory 8 GB (4 GB minimum) Processors 4 Disk 80 GB, growing (\"thin\", \"dynamically allocated\") Firmware BIOS or UEFI. Secure Boot off. Graphics the standard adapter; no 3D acceleration needed CPU model pass through the host CPU (see below) Pass through the processor Blender 5, appleseed, Mitsuba and LuxCore need SSE4.2 and AVX. Some VM programs show the guest a basic processor without them. Choose \"host-passthrough\", \"host\" or \"copy host CPU configuration\". QEMU (command line) qemu-img create -f qcow2 animux.qcow2 80G qemu-system-x86_64 -enable-kvm -cpu host -m 8192 -smp 4 \\ -machine q35 -vga std -nic user,model=e1000 \\ -cdrom animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso -drive file=animux.qcow2,if=virtio \\ -boot d -usb -device usb-tablet On Windows replace -enable-kvm with -accel whpx,kernel-irqchip=off , on macOS with -accel hvf . Add UEFI with -bios /usr/share/ovmf/OVMF.fd (Linux; path may differ). virt-manager (Linux, also included in Animux) File → New Virtual Machine → Local install media → choose the ISO → operating system Debian 12 → memory 8192, CPUs 4 → disk 80 GB → tick Customize configuration before install → CPUs : tick Copy host CPU configuration (host-passthrough) → Begin Installation . VMware Workstation Player / Fusion (Intel Mac) Create a New Virtual Machine → Installer disc image file (iso) → guest Linux, Debian 12.x 64-bit → disk 80 GB, store as a single file → Customize Hardware : memory 8 GB, 4 processors. VMware passes the CPU through by default. Hyper-V (Windows Pro) Create a Generation 2 VM with 8192 MB startup memory (untick Dynamic Memory ), connect it to the Default Switch , create an 80 GB disk and choose the ISO. Then in Settings → Security untick Enable Secure Boot , and in Processor set 4 virtual processors. Apple Silicon Macs (M1–M4) Animux is built for Intel/AMD (x86-64) processors. On an Apple Silicon Mac it can only run through emulation (UTM in \"Emulate\" mode), which is far too slow for animation work. Use a PC instead.", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Make a bootable USB stick", "s": "Step by step - write the Animux ISO to a USB stick with Rufus, balenaEtcher or Ventoy, start a computer from it, and fix the usual problems.", "u": "start/usb-stick.html", "x": "From a USB stick, Animux runs at full speed on your real computer and uses its real graphics card, without changing anything on the hard disk. Making the stick takes about half an hour, most of it waiting. Before you start A USB stick of 16 GB or more. The ISO file is 12 GB. A USB 3 stick (often blue inside the plug) is much faster, both to write and to run from. Everything on the stick is erased. Copy anything you want to keep onto your computer first. Unplug other USB drives and memory cards , so you cannot pick the wrong one by mistake. The ISO file animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso ( Get the ISO ). Optional: check the ISO is not damaged A download that broke halfway gives a stick that does not start. On Windows, open PowerShell (right-click the Start button → Terminal ) and type, with your own path: Get-FileHash \"C:\\Users\\you\\Downloads\\animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso\" -Algorithm SHA256 Compare the long code with the SHA-256 published next to the download. On macOS use shasum -a 256 file.iso , on Linux sha256sum file.iso . Pick one of the three programs below. On Windows, Rufus is recommended. Option A: Rufus (Windows, recommended) Download Rufus from https://rufus.ie/ . The Portable version needs no installation. Start it and answer Yes when Windows asks for permission. Device : choose your USB stick. Check the size, so you do not pick another disk. Boot selection : click SELECT and choose animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso . Leave Partition scheme , Target system and everything else as Rufus fills it in. Click START . Rufus says \"ISOHybrid image detected\" . Choose Write in DD Image mode and click OK . Confirm that the stick will be erased. Writing takes 10 to 30 minutes . When the bar says READY , click CLOSE . DD Image mode is important The ISO contains files bigger than 4 GB. The other choice, ISO Image mode , cannot copy them, and the stick then does not start. Windows says: You need to format the disk After writing, Windows may say You need to format the disk in drive E: before you can use it . Click Cancel . This is normal: Windows cannot read the stick's Linux format. Formatting would erase Animux again. Option B: balenaEtcher (Windows, macOS, Linux, simplest) Download balenaEtcher from https://etcher.balena.io/ , install it and start it. Flash from file → choose animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso . Select target → choose your USB stick. Check the size. Flash! Wait until it says Flash Complete (10 to 30 minutes). Etcher checks the stick for errors by itself after writing. On macOS, click Ignore if macOS says it cannot read the disk; on Windows click Cancel at the \"format the disk\" message, as above. Option C: Ventoy (several ISO files on one stick) Ventoy is useful when you want to keep several builds or other systems on the same stick. Download Ventoy from https://www.ventoy.net/ , unpack it and start Ventoy2Disk.exe . Choose the stick and click Install . This erases the stick once. Copy animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso onto the stick like a normal file (Explorer or Finder). Later versions can sit beside it. When you start from the stick, Ventoy shows a list. Choose the Animux ISO, then Boot in normal mode . Start the computer from the stick Plug the stick in and switch the computer on, or restart it. Straight away, press the boot menu key a few times. It depends on the maker: Maker Boot menu key Acer, Asus (laptops), Dell, Lenovo (most), Toshiba F12 Asus (desktops), MSI F8 or F11 HP F9 (or Esc , then F9) Gigabyte, ASRock F12 or F11 Lenovo with a \"Novo\" button press the small Novo button while off Apple Mac (Intel) hold ⌥ Option while starting Choose the USB stick. It may be listed twice, once as UEFI: … and once without. Either works; UEFI is preferred. The Animux boot menu appears. Press Enter . The desktop appears after one to three minutes. From Windows 10 or 11, without a boot key Hold Shift while you click Start → Power → Restart . In the blue menu choose Use a device and then your USB stick. Apple Macs with an M1, M2 or later chip (Appl", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "The boot menu", "s": "What each entry in the Animux start menu does, and which one to choose when the screen stays black.", "u": "start/boot-menu.html", "x": "When Animux starts from the ISO or USB stick you see this menu. Use the arrow keys to choose and Enter to start. If you press nothing, the first entry starts after 10 seconds. The Animux boot menu (GRUB). Entry What it does When to use it Animator Workstation Starts the full desktop and logs in the live user automatically. Normally. Render Farm Master Switches on the services a render farm head node uses: DHCP server and NFS file sharing. Starts to a text login without a desktop (in r16 it still showed the desktop). Only on a computer that will hand out render jobs. See Render farm roles . Render Farm Slave A render node, started to a text login (in r16 still with the desktop). Only on extra computers that only render. Install Animux Genesis64 to Disk Starts the desktop and opens the installer by itself. To install Animux . Workstation (KMS / no nomodeset) Desktop with the graphics card's own driver from the first second. If the normal entry gives a black screen or a very low resolution on a real computer. Workstation (verbose / no Plymouth) Desktop, showing all start-up messages. To see where starting hangs. DEBUG: Text Console Text login only (user user , password user ). Repairs and fault finding. Boot from first hard disk (EFI) Leaves Animux and starts the system on the hard disk. Shown only on UEFI computers. GRUB command line The boot loader's command prompt. Experts only. Black screen? Wait two minutes. The first start in a VM or from a slow USB stick takes time. Restart and choose Workstation (KMS / no nomodeset) . Still black: choose verbose and look at the last lines, or see Troubleshooting . Adding your own options Press e on an entry to edit it for one start. You can add words to the line that begins with linux , for example toram (copies the whole system into memory so you can remove the USB stick; needs 16 GB RAM or more). Press Ctrl+X or F10 to start. Nothing is saved. After installation An installed Animux has a shorter menu with Animux and its recovery mode. It shows the Animux splash screen while it starts and then the login screen: Animux starting from the hard disk.", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "The live session", "s": "Animux running straight from the USB stick or ISO. The user name and password, what is kept, what is lost, and how to save work.", "u": "start/live-session.html", "x": "\"Live\" means Animux runs straight from the ISO or USB stick, without being installed. It is the full system with every program. User name user (shown as Animator ) Password user Administrator ( sudo ) allowed without a password Computer name animux You are logged in automatically. The password is only needed if a program asks for it, for example when the screen locks. What is kept and what is lost Everything you save in the live session disappears when you switch off or restart. This includes files in the Home folder, settings, and programs you install. The system keeps them in memory (RAM) , which is also why big projects can fill the memory. To keep your work: Plug in a USB stick or USB disk . An icon appears on the desktop, and the drive is listed on the left in the file manager . Save your project directly onto that drive , or copy it there before you switch off. Before you pull the drive out, right-click its desktop icon and choose Eject (or Unmount ), so that everything is really written. Or install Animux so that everything stays. The disks of the computer The computer's own disks appear in the file manager too. You can open files from them. Be careful: Animux can also change and delete files there. If Windows was not shut down completely (Fast Startup), its disk may be \"read-only\". Leaving the live session Animux menu → Log Out → Shut Down . On a real computer, remove the USB stick when the screen goes black.", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Install Animux on a disk", "s": "Step-by-step through the installer, with screenshots, for a whole disk, a virtual disk or next to Windows.", "u": "start/install.html", "x": "Installing copies Animux onto a disk, so it starts without the USB stick and keeps your files and settings . It takes 15–40 minutes. Back up first Installing can erase a disk. Copy everything important from the computer to another drive first. In a virtual machine this does not matter: the installer only sees the empty virtual disk. Before you start A disk (or free space) of at least 40 GB ; 100 GB or more is better. The computer plugged in to power. For dual boot with Windows : shrink Windows first, in Windows: Disk Management → right-click C: → Shrink Volume , and free at least 60 GB. Also turn off Windows Fast Startup and, if it is on, BitLocker for the time of the installation. 1. Start the installer Start from the USB stick or ISO and choose Install Animux Genesis64 to Disk in the boot menu . The desktop starts and the installer opens by itself. (The installer can also be started from a normal live session: open a terminal and type sudo -E calamares .) 2. Welcome, location, keyboard The installer's first page. The steps are listed on the left. Welcome : choose your language, click Next . Location : click your region on the map, or choose region and city. This sets the clock and the date format. Keyboard : choose your keyboard layout. Type in the test box to check that the keys are right (especially @ , \" and / ). 3. Partitions: where Animux goes Choosing the disk. In a virtual machine \"Erase disk\" only erases the virtual disk. At the top, Select storage device shows the disk. Make sure it is the right one: check its size. Option Use it when Erase disk The whole disk is for Animux (new disk, virtual machine). Everything on the disk is deleted. Install alongside Windows or another system is on the disk and you want to choose at every start. Drag the divider to give Animux its share. Only shown when there is room. Replace a partition You prepared an empty partition for Animux. Manual partitioning You know exactly which partitions you want. For Erase disk you can also choose Swap (extra memory on disk; Swap (with Hibernate) lets you hibernate) and tick Encrypt system to protect a laptop with a password at every start. 4. Users Your account: name, login name, computer name and password. Field Example Note What is your name? Ada Lovelace shown on the login screen Login name ada lower case, no spaces Computer name ada-studio how the computer is called on the network; use different names on render farm computers Password (twice) You need it to log in and to install software. Write it down. Log in automatically Tick it only on a computer nobody else uses. 5. Summary and installation The summary. Nothing has been changed yet; \"Install\" starts the real work. Read the summary. Nothing has been changed yet. Click Install , then Install now . Installing. A slideshow runs while the files are copied; this is the longest part. Finished. Tick Restart now and click Done. When it says All done , tick Restart now and click Done . Remove the USB stick when the screen goes black (in VirtualBox: remove the ISO under Settings → Storage ). 6. First start from the disk Animux shows its splash screen, then the login screen. Type your password and press Enter . The login screen of an installed Animux. Continue with First steps after installing . Dual boot: choosing Windows or Animux With Install alongside , every start shows a menu where you choose Animux or Windows Boot Manager . If Windows starts straight away without a menu, open your computer's boot menu (F12 etc., see USB stick ) or set debian first in the firmware's boot order.", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "First steps after installing", "s": "A ten-minute checklist for a freshly installed Animux, covering updates, the screen, the self-test, a project folder and backups.", "u": "start/first-steps.html", "x": "Log in with the name and password you chose in the installer. Connect to the internet : click the network icon at the top right (Wi-Fi) or plug in a cable. Update the system. Open the terminal and type: sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade Screen : Settings → Display . Set the resolution, and the position of a second monitor. See Settings . Keyboard layout : Settings → Keyboard → Layout , if the installer's choice is not right. Run the self-test : Animux Tools → Animux Self-Test . Every line should say PASS. See Self-test . Graphics driver (NVIDIA cards): install NVIDIA's driver for fast 3D. See Adding software . Make a projects folder : ~/Projects , with the folder layout studios use. Plan backups : an external disk with a copy of ~/Projects , and a Timeshift snapshot of the system ( Digital Asset Managers → Timeshift ). Make something : start with Your first 3D render or Hand-drawn animation . Differences from the live session Live session Installed Files and settings lost at shutdown kept Login automatic as user your own account and password sudo no password your password Start-up text, then desktop Animux splash screen, then login screen Graphics driver safe mode on BIOS computers the card's own driver Speed depends on the USB stick full disk speed", "sec": "Getting Started"}, {"t": "Desktop tour", "s": "The top panel, the Animux menu, the dock and the desktop icons, explained one by one.", "u": "desktop/tour.html", "x": "Animux uses the Xfce desktop. If you know Windows, most things work the same way: windows, a menu, double-click to open, right-click for more options. The Animux desktop. The top panel Where What How to use it Top left: Animux The menu with every program Click it, then move the mouse over a category. See The Animux menu . Next to it Open windows Click a window's name to bring it to the front or hide it. Middle right Workspaces (four small boxes) Four separate desktops. Click one to switch; handy to keep Blender on one and the browser on another. Right Network, volume, notifications Click the network icon for Wi-Fi, the speaker for volume. Right Date and time Click for a calendar. Far right: Animator Your user name Log out, restart, shut down, lock the screen. The dock (bottom) Icon What it does Blue rectangle Show desktop : hides all windows (click again to bring them back). Black box with >_ Terminal : type commands ( how ). Grey filing drawer File manager : your files and drives ( how ). Blue globe Web browser (Firefox). Green rocket Application finder : type a few letters of a program's name and press Enter. Blue folder Folder menu : shows the folders in your Home as a menu, so you can open one quickly. Desktop icons Trash : deleted files go here first. Right-click → Empty Trash to delete them for good. File System : the whole system. You rarely need it. Home : your personal folder with Documents, Pictures, Videos and so on. USB sticks and disks appear here when you plug them in. Starting programs: three ways The menu : Animux → category → program. Search : press Alt+F2 or click the green rocket, type for example blender , press Enter . Double-click a file : a .blend file opens in Blender, a picture in the image viewer, a video in VLC. Windows Drag a window by its title bar. Double-click the title bar to make it fill the screen. The buttons at the top right of each window: minimise (_), maximise (□), close (×). Alt+Tab switches between windows. Drag a window to the left or right edge of the screen to give it half the screen. Shutting down Click Animator at the top right (or Animux → Log Out ) and choose Shut Down or Restart . Live session In the live session, files are lost when you shut down. See The live session . This guide on Animux The guide is installed on Animux and works without internet: Animux → Animux Tools → Animux Guide . It also opens in the web browser at http://127.0.0.1:8765/ , and it opens by itself the first time you log in. This guide in Firefox on Animux (dark theme).", "sec": "Using the Desktop"}, {"t": "The Animux menu", "s": "A map of every menu category, so you know where to find the program for each job in an animation production.", "u": "desktop/menu.html", "x": "Click Animux at the top left. The menu is sorted by production job , the way the original Animux was, not by program type. The menu with 3D Animation open: Modellers, Pipeline Tools, Renderers and Render Queue Managers. Category Inside Most useful 2D Animation 2D animation programs Pencil2D , Synfig Studio , TupiTube 3D Animation → Modellers 3D modelling and character programs Blender , MakeHuman 2 , FreeCAD, Wings 3D, MeshLab 3D Animation → Pipeline Tools Studio scene and format tools Gaffer , USD View 3D Animation → Renderers Renderers with a window POV-Ray, appleseed (others: see Renderers ) 3D Animation → Render Queue Managers Render farm Flamenco Manager , Farmerjoe StopMotion Animation Frame-by-frame camera capture Stopmotion 2D Graphics → Bitmap Editors Painting and photo editing Krita , GIMP , MyPaint, mtPaint, GrafX2 2D Graphics → Vector Editors Drawing with shapes Inkscape 2D Graphics → Image Viewers Look at pictures and renders Ristretto, Image Viewer, DJV 2D Graphics → Ancillary Tools Other graphics tools Upscayl , colour calibration, scanning NLV Editors Video editing Kdenlive , OpenShot, Cinelerra-GG, Avidemux DVD Authoring → Production Converting and finishing video HandBrake, MKVToolNix, subtitle editors DVD Authoring → Backup Burning discs K3b Sound Engineering Recording, music, audio setup Audacity, Ardour, LMMS, Hydrogen, MuseScore , QjackCtl, volume control Media Players Watch and listen VLC, Parole Publishing Storyboards, PDF, print Scribus , PDF Arranger, Document Viewer Screen Casting Screenshots Screenshot Digital Asset Managers Find and manage files and backups File manager, Catfish search, Disk Usage Analyzer, Timeshift Animux Tools Animux's own tools Animux Guide, Self-Test, Sample Scenes, Install DaVinci Resolve Accessories, Education, Internet, Office, System, Settings The usual desktop programs Calculator, text editor, Firefox, Synaptic, Settings Manager Can't find a program? Press Alt+F2 and type part of its name, or use the software list , which says where each program is in the menu.", "sec": "Using the Desktop"}, {"t": "Files, folders and drives", "s": "Where your work is stored, how to use the file manager, USB drives and network shares, and a sensible folder layout for animation projects.", "u": "desktop/files.html", "x": "Your Home folder Every user has a Home folder ( /home/your-login-name , written ~ for short). It is like My Documents on Windows: everything of yours goes here. Folder Use Desktop files shown on the desktop Documents scripts, notes, storyboards Pictures images, textures, renders (POV-Ray GUI saves to Pictures/povray ) Videos finished films Music soundtracks Downloads what Firefox downloads animux-selftest results of the self-test The file manager Click the grey filing drawer in the dock, or the Home icon on the desktop. The file manager (Thunar) showing the Animux sample scenes. Left side : places (Home, Desktop, Trash), drives and network. Double-click a file to open it; right-click → Open With to choose another program. Copy and paste : Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V. Rename : F2. Delete : Delete key (goes to the Trash). Show hidden files (their names start with a dot, for example settings folders): Ctrl+H . New folder : right-click on empty space → Create Folder . Open a terminal here : right-click → Open Terminal Here . USB sticks and disks Plug them in: an icon appears on the desktop and the drive is listed on the left in the file manager. Always eject before unplugging : right-click the drive → Eject (or Safely Remove ), and wait until the icon disappears. Otherwise files can be damaged. Drives formatted for Windows (NTFS, exFAT, FAT32) and Mac (exFAT) work. For drives used only with Linux, ext4 is best; you can format a drive with System → Disks (this erases it). Network folders In the file manager, click Network on the left, or type an address in the location bar (Ctrl+L): Kind Address Windows share / NAS smb://server-name/share Another Linux computer over SSH sftp://user@computer/home/user NFS (render farm) nfs://server/path A folder layout for an animation project Studios keep every project in the same shape so everybody (and every render node) finds things. A simple version: ~/Projects/my-short/ ├── 01-story/ script, storyboard (Scribus, Krita), animatic ├── 02-design/ character and set designs ├── 03-assets/ models, textures, rigs (.blend, .png, .exr) ├── 04-shots/ one .blend per shot: sh010.blend, sh020.blend … ├── 05-renders/ sh010/0001.png … one folder per shot ├── 06-sound/ dialogue, effects, music └── 07-edit/ Kdenlive project and final MP4 Use lower-case names without spaces ( sh010-walk.blend , not Shot 10 Walk.blend ). Command-line tools and render farms like them better. Use relative paths in Blender ( File → External Data → Make Paths Relative ) so the project still works when copied to another computer. Back up Copy your project folder to an external drive regularly. On an installed system, Timeshift ( Digital Asset Managers → Timeshift ) can also snapshot the system before big changes. See System tools .", "sec": "Using the Desktop"}, {"t": "The terminal, gently", "s": "The twelve commands an animator actually needs, how to copy and paste them, and how sudo works.", "u": "desktop/terminal.html", "x": "The terminal is a window where you type instructions instead of clicking. You can do almost everything on Animux without it. It is useful for a few jobs: rendering in the background, converting many files at once, and fixing problems. This guide always gives the full command; you can copy and paste it. Open it with the >_ icon in the dock, or Animux → Terminal Emulator . A terminal running the Animux self-test. Copy and paste In the terminal Elsewhere Copy Ctrl+Shift+C Ctrl+C Paste Ctrl+Shift+V (or the middle mouse button) Ctrl+V Press Enter to run a command. Ctrl+C stops a running command. The Up arrow brings back the previous command. Tab completes file names. The twelve useful commands Command What it does Example ls list the files here ls ~/Pictures cd go into a folder cd ~/Projects/my-short pwd show where you are pwd cp copy cp shot.blend shot-backup.blend mv move or rename mv render.png final.png mkdir make a folder mkdir -p ~/Projects/my-short/05-renders rm delete for good (no Trash!) rm old-test.png xdg-open open a file with its usual program xdg-open final.mp4 df -h how full the disks are df -h free -h how much memory is used free -h htop live list of running programs (q quits) htop man the manual of a command (q quits) man ffmpeg Programs from the terminal Every program in the menu can be started by name, for example blender , krita or kdenlive . Add & to keep using the terminal: krita & . The software list shows each program's command. Rendering without opening a window is the most useful trick: blender -b ~/Projects/my-short/04-shots/sh010.blend -a See Command-line rendering . sudo: doing things as administrator Installing software and changing the system need administrator rights. Put sudo in front: sudo apt update On an installed system it asks for your password (nothing appears while you type; that is normal). In the live session it does not ask. Warning Only use sudo with commands you understand or that come from this guide. As administrator you can damage the system. Paths, quickly ~ is your Home folder; ~/Pictures is the Pictures folder in it. . is \"here\", .. is \"one folder up\". Names with spaces need quotes: cd \"~/My Projects\" – or avoid spaces. Upper and lower case are different: Render.png and render.png are two files.", "sec": "Using the Desktop"}, {"t": "Settings", "s": "Screen resolution, several monitors, keyboard layout, mouse and tablet, sound, language, Wi-Fi, printers and power.", "u": "desktop/settings.html", "x": "All settings are in Animux → Settings → Settings Manager . The Settings Manager. Screen resolution and several monitors Settings → Display . Choose the Resolution and click Apply ; keep it if the picture is good (otherwise it returns after a few seconds). With two monitors, drag them into the right positions and choose which one is the Primary Display . Display settings. Only a few resolutions in a VM or on a new graphics card? The normal start entry uses a simple, safe graphics mode on BIOS computers. Start with Workstation (KMS / no nomodeset) in the boot menu to use the graphics card's own driver, which offers every resolution. An installed system uses the card's driver automatically. Colour accuracy (for painting and grading): calibrate with a colorimeter and DisplayCAL ( 2D Graphics → Ancillary Tools ). Keyboard layout Settings → Keyboard → Layout : untick Use system defaults , click + Add and choose your layout. Several layouts can be switched with the key combination chosen there. Mouse, touchpad and drawing tablet Settings → Mouse and Touchpad : speed, left-handed buttons, tap-to-click. A three-button mouse is strongly recommended for Blender. On a laptop, tick Emulate 3 Button Mouse in Blender's preferences. Drawing tablets work with pressure in Krita, GIMP, MyPaint and Blender. Set the pressure curve inside each program (Krita: Settings → Configure Krita → Tablet settings ). Sound The speaker icon in the panel changes the volume. Sound Engineering → Sound Server Configuration → PulseAudio Volume Control chooses which output (speakers, HDMI, headphones) and input (microphone) are used, per program. Audio for recording is explained in Sound and music . Language, time and region Time zone: right-click the clock → Properties , or on an installed system run sudo dpkg-reconfigure tzdata . Language of the menus: on an installed system run sudo dpkg-reconfigure locales , choose your language (for example de_DE.UTF-8 ), then log out and in. Network and Wi-Fi Click the network icon at the top right, choose the Wi-Fi network and type its password. Edit Connections there for fixed addresses (useful for render farm computers). Printers and scanners Settings → Print Settings adds printers (most network printers are found automatically). Scanners work with Simple Scan ( Graphics → Document Scanner ). Power, screen saver and lock Settings → Power Manager : when the screen turns off and when the computer sleeps. Tip for long renders: set Put to sleep when inactive to Never on the System tab, otherwise the computer may sleep in the middle of a render. Appearance Settings → Appearance (theme, icons, fonts) and right-click the desktop → Desktop Settings for the wallpaper. Animux comes with its own wallpapers in /usr/share/backgrounds/animux .", "sec": "Using the Desktop"}, {"t": "Updates and adding software", "s": "Keep Animux up to date and install more programs with Synaptic, apt or Flatpak, including DaVinci Resolve and the NVIDIA driver.", "u": "desktop/add-software.html", "x": "Installed systems only In the live session you can install software too, but it is gone after a restart. Updates Animux gets software from two places: Debian 12 : security and bug-fix updates for the base system and most programs. The Animux repository : Animux's own packages (Blender 5, Gaffer, the renderers, the menus, this guide…). It is signed with the Animux Archive Signing Key . In r16 it is switched off ( /etc/apt/sources.list.d/animux.sources , Enabled: no ) until it is online; Animux packages are then updated with the next release. To update, open a terminal and type: sudo apt update sudo apt upgrade Answer Y when asked. Restart if the kernel was updated. Synaptic: install with a list System → Synaptic Package Manager . Click Search , type a name (for example natron or obs ), right-click the package → Mark for Installation → Apply . apt: install with one command sudo apt install obs-studio apt search word finds packages, sudo apt remove name removes one. Flatpak and Flathub: newer versions Flathub has newer versions of many creative programs (Krita, OBS Studio, OpenToonz, Natron, Inkscape…). Set it up once: flatpak remote-add --if-not-exists --user flathub https://dl.flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo Then install, for example OpenToonz (2D animation used by Studio Ghibli; already installed on Animux r16.3 and later): flatpak install --user flathub io.github.OpenToonz Flatpak programs appear in the menu after you log out and in. DaVinci Resolve Animux Tools → Install DaVinci Resolve opens Blackmagic Design's download page. Resolve is free to download but not open source, so Animux cannot include it. It needs a strong NVIDIA or AMD graphics card with the vendor's driver. NVIDIA driver For fast 3D and GPU rendering with an NVIDIA card, install NVIDIA's own driver from Debian's non-free section: sudo apt install nvidia-driver firmware-misc-nonfree Restart. Check with nvidia-smi . Cycles can then render on the GPU ( Blender → Edit → Preferences → System → CUDA or OptiX ). Warning Do not install drivers downloaded as .run files from nvidia.com at the same time; use one or the other. What not to do Do not add software sources (\"PPAs\") made for Ubuntu ; they can break Debian. Do not remove packages whose names start with animux- unless you know why: menus, branding and the start-up roles depend on them.", "sec": "Using the Desktop"}, {"t": "Keyboard shortcuts", "s": "A cheat sheet for the desktop and for the shortcuts animators use most in Blender, Krita, Pencil2D and Kdenlive.", "u": "desktop/shortcuts.html", "x": "Desktop (Xfce) Keys Does Alt+F2 run a program by name Alt+Tab switch windows Alt+F4 close the window Alt+F10 maximise / restore the window Ctrl+Alt+D show the desktop Ctrl+Alt+T open a terminal Ctrl+Alt+L lock the screen Ctrl+Alt+Delete log out, restart or shut down Ctrl+Alt+← / → previous / next workspace Print screenshot (full screen); Alt+Print the window Ctrl+Esc open the program menu at the mouse pointer Blender Keys Does Middle mouse drag orbit the view; Shift +middle: pan; wheel: zoom Numpad 0 look through the camera G / R / S grab (move), rotate, scale; then X, Y or Z to lock an axis Shift+A add an object Tab edit mode ↔ object mode I insert a keyframe Space play / stop the animation F12 render the picture; Ctrl+F12 render the animation N side panel (MPFB is a tab here) Ctrl+S save Ctrl+Z undo F3 search for any command Krita Keys Does B brush; E toggles eraser mode [ / ] smaller / bigger brush Space +drag pan; Ctrl+Space +drag: zoom Insert new layer Ctrl+Z undo Pencil2D Keys Does N pencil, P pen, B brush, E eraser, K paint bucket tools , / . previous / next frame Enter play / stop Kdenlive Keys Does Space play / pause J / K / L backwards / pause / forwards I / O mark in / out point in the clip monitor Shift+R cut the clip at the playhead Ctrl+Enter render (export)", "sec": "Using the Desktop"}, {"t": "All software", "s": "Every program on Animux by job, with what it does and the command that starts it.", "u": "software/index.html", "x": "Animux has more than a hundred programs. This list has the ones that matter for animation, grouped by job. The name links to a longer page where there is one. Start it is what you type after Alt+F2 or in a terminal ; every program is also in the menu . Where to begin New to animation software? Try these first: Pencil2D (drawing), Blender (3D), Kdenlive (editing) and Audacity (sound). 3D modelling and animation Menu: 3D Animation → Modellers . Program What it does Start it Blender The complete 3D studio: modelling, sculpting, rigging, animation, simulation, rendering (Cycles and EEVEE), compositing and video editing. blender MPFB (MakeHuman Plugin for Blender) Creates realistic human characters, ready to animate, directly inside Blender. Switched on automatically. blender Maya Workflow Kit for Blender Makes Blender work like Autodesk Maya for animators trained on it: Maya hotkeys, marking menus, shelf, Channel Box and layout. Off until you switch it on. animux-blender-maya on MakeHuman II Stand-alone character creator: shape a human body with sliders, add clothes, a skeleton and poses, then export for Blender or games. makehuman2 FreeCAD Precise CAD modelling for props, sets and objects that must have exact sizes. freecad OpenSCAD Build 3D objects by writing a short description instead of drawing them. openscad Wings 3D Small, friendly polygon modeller, good for learning how 3D meshes are built. wings3d MeshLab Clean up, repair and simplify 3D scans and heavy meshes. meshlab Geomview Viewer for mathematical 3D shapes (OFF files). geomview Renderers Programs that turn a 3D scene into a picture. Menu: 3D Animation → Renderers (several are command-line programs only). Program What it does Start it Cycles and EEVEE (inside Blender) Blender's own renderers. Cycles is realistic (path tracing); EEVEE is fast (real time). Start here. blender POV-Ray Classic ray tracer: you describe the scene in a text file and POV-Ray draws it. The menu entry asks for a .pov file and shows the picture. animux-povray-gui appleseed Physically based production renderer. Command line only on Animux (its editor window needs Python 2.7, which Debian 12 no longer has). appleseed.cli LuxCoreRender Unbiased physically based renderer, good for realistic light, glass and caustics. Used from Python scripts. animux-vfx-python Mitsuba 3 Research renderer, also able to compute how a picture changes when the scene changes (differentiable rendering). mitsuba pbrt-v4 The renderer from the book \"Physically Based Rendering\". Ideal for learning how renderers work. pbrt Pipeline tools and file formats For studios and advanced users: scene assembly, interchange formats and colour management. Menu: 3D Animation → Pipeline Tools . Program What it does Start it Gaffer Node-based tool for look development, lighting and scene assembly, used in film studios. Renders with Cycles. Includes Cortex, OSL, OpenUSD and Alembic tools. gaffer OpenUSD tools (usdview, usdcat …) Universal Scene Description: the scene format shared by Blender, Houdini, Maya and game engines. usdview shows a USD file; usdcat, usdchecker, usdtree inspect and convert. usdview Alembic tools (abcls, abctree …) Alembic stores baked animation (moving geometry) to move it between programs. abcls Open Shading Language (oslc, oslinfo, testshade) Language for writing your own materials (shaders); works in Blender Cycles and Gaffer. oslc OpenEXR and OpenImageIO tools EXR is the high-quality image format of film work (many layers, real light values). oiiotool converts, resizes and combines images; exrheader shows what is inside. oiiotool OpenColorIO tools Colour management: keeps colours identical across Blender, Krita, Gaffer and your screen. ociocheck OpenVDB tools Format for smoke, fire, clouds and other volumes. vdb_print Animux VFX Python A ready Python 3.11 with Mitsuba, LuxCore, OpenUSD, OpenEXR, OpenColorIO, OpenImageIO, MaterialX and NumPy installed together. animux-vfx-python Render farm Spread rendering over sev", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Blender", "s": "The 3D studio at the heart of Animux. Blender 5.2 LTS with Cycles, EEVEE, Grease Pencil and the MPFB character add-on, and how to find your way around it.", "u": "software/blender.html", "x": "Blender Menu 3D Animation → Modellers → Blender Version 5.2.3 LTS Command blender Good for Beginner to professional Files .blend; imports/exports FBX, OBJ, glTF, USD, Alembic, STL, PLY, SVG Licence GPL-3.0-or-later Website blender.org Self-test animux-selftest blender Blender does almost everything in a 3D film: modelling, sculpting, texturing, rigging, animation, physics (cloth, smoke, fluids), rendering, compositing and even video editing . It is used for feature films, TV series, games and advertising. Animux includes the official Blender 5.2.3 LTS from blender.org (\"LTS\" = long-term support, with bug fixes until 2028), not the old Blender 3.4 that Debian 12 ships. Start Blender Animux → 3D Animation → Modellers → Blender , or type blender . The first time, a welcome screen (the splash ) appears; click anywhere outside it to close it. Blender's splash screen. \"General\" starts an empty 3D scene, \"2D Animation\" a Grease Pencil drawing, \"Video Editing\" the video editor. The screen The Blender window: the 3D viewport in the middle, the outliner (list of objects) at the top right, the properties editor below it and the timeline at the bottom. Part Where What for Top bar top menus ( File , Render …) and workspaces ( Layout , Modeling , Animation , Shading …): ready-made screen layouts for each job 3D Viewport middle your scene. Middle mouse button to orbit, wheel to zoom Toolbar left of the viewport select, move, rotate, scale… Outliner top right every object in the scene (camera, light, cube) Properties bottom right settings, in tabs: render, output, object, modifiers, materials… Timeline bottom frames, play button, keyframes Your first hour Follow the tutorials in order: Your first 3D render : object, material, light, camera, render, save. A turntable animation : keyframes and a video file. A character with MakeHuman : a human with a skeleton, in Blender. From idea to finished film : how the steps fit together. Render engines Engine Use it for Speed EEVEE previews, stylised and cartoon looks, motion graphics seconds per frame on a good graphics card Cycles realistic light, glass, reflections seconds to minutes per frame Workbench quick clay previews instant Choose in Properties → Render → Render Engine . On computers without a strong graphics card (and in virtual machines), Cycles on the CPU is the most reliable choice. EEVEE needs OpenGL 4.3; in a VM it runs through software emulation and is slow. GPU rendering in Cycles needs NVIDIA's driver (CUDA/OptiX) or AMD's HIP. See Adding software , then Edit → Preferences → System . Grease Pencil: 2D inside Blender File → New → 2D Animation gives a drawing canvas with layers, onion skin and a timeline. Draw with a tablet, animate frame by frame, and combine with 3D objects. Add-ons included MPFB 2.0.18 (MakeHuman Plugin for Blender): the MPFB tab in the side panel (press N in the viewport) creates human characters. It is switched on automatically. See MakeHuman and MPFB . Maya Workflow Kit 0.7.0 (new in r16.3, off until you switch it on): see Coming from Maya below. Blender's own extensions ( Edit → Preferences → Get Extensions ) can be installed from extensions.blender.org when you are online. Coming from Maya Trained on Autodesk Maya? The Maya Workflow Kit (by ohnsk, free, GPL-3.0) makes Blender work like Maya: Maya hotkeys and Alt+mouse navigation, QWER tools, the Space hotbox and marking menus, the shelf , the Channel Box , the Outliner with persp/top/front/side, the time slider and a Maya-style command line. It contains no Autodesk code. It is installed but off , because it changes Blender's keys and layout. To switch it on: Close Blender. Menu Animux Tools → Blender: Maya Workflow Kit on/off , answer y (or type animux-blender-maya on in a terminal ). Start Blender. The kit's welcome window says that nothing has changed yet; click Make Blender feel like Maya . In Blender, the Maya | Blender switch at the top right (or Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M ) changes between the Maya layout and Blender", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "MakeHuman II and MPFB", "s": "Make realistic human characters with sliders, either in the stand-alone MakeHuman II or with the MPFB add-on directly inside Blender.", "u": "software/makehuman.html", "x": "MakeHuman II Menu 3D Animation → Modellers → MakeHuman 2 Version 2.0.1 Command makehuman2 Good for Beginner Files .mhm; exports GLB (glTF), OBJ, STL, MH2B (Blender), BVH (poses) Licence AGPL-3.0 Website github.com/makehumancommunity/makehuman2 Self-test animux-selftest apps MakeHuman is the classic free character creator: you move sliders (age, gender, weight, height, face shape…) and get a clean, animation-ready human body. The project has two current tools, and Animux includes both: MakeHuman II MPFB What the stand-alone program the same character system as a Blender add-on Version on Animux 2.0.1 (\"Fantasy\") 2.0.18 Start 3D Animation → Modellers → MakeHuman 2 in Blender: press N → tab MPFB Best for designing a character, exporting to games or other programs characters you animate in Blender About the name The original MakeHuman 1.x (Python 2) is no longer developed. Its successor is MakeHuman II ( makehumancommunity/makehuman2 on GitHub). MPFB ( MakeHuman Plugin For Blender ) is maintained by the same community. Both use the same assets. MakeHuman II MakeHuman II. The two rows of icons at the top left choose the task (hold the mouse over an icon to see its name); the panel below changes with it. The character is in the viewport, with camera buttons on the right. The very first start: MakeHuman II asks where to keep your characters and downloads. Keep the suggestion and click Save. Start MakeHuman 2 . The very first time, it asks for a personal folder (suggested: ~/makehuman2 ); click Save . Then it shows Base mesh :: selection : keep fantasy (the MakeHuman II body; hm08 is the classic MakeHuman 1 body) and click Select . Click the modify character icon. The left side lists categories ( Modify character :: categories ) such as main proportions, head, torso, arms and legs. Open one and move its sliders: gender, age, muscle and weight change the whole body. The summary at the bottom right shows age, muscle, weight and height. Use the other icons to add clothes, hair, eyebrows, eyes , a skeleton and a pose or expression. Use the camera buttons on the right of the viewport to look from the front, side or top, or to fit the face. Save the character as .mhm (a small file you can change later). Export as GLB (glTF, for games, the web and Blender), OBJ , STL (3D printing), MH2B (for Blender with the included io_makehuman add-on) or a pose/animation as BVH . Small screen Some MakeHuman II dialogs are taller than 800 pixels. On a small screen, move a dialog by holding Alt and dragging it with the mouse. MPFB inside Blender A human created with MPFB. The MPFB tab is the last vertical tab at the right edge of the side panel. Start Blender, move the mouse over the 3D viewport and press N . Click the MPFB tab at the right edge. Open New human → From scratch and click Create human . Open Model and change the sliders, as in MakeHuman. Rigging → Standard rig → Add standard rig (or Rigify ) gives it a skeleton you can pose and animate. Apply assets adds skin, eyes, eyebrows, hair and clothes from asset packs. MPFB can also load a .mhm file saved by MakeHuman II ( New human → From save file ), or take the character straight from a running MakeHuman II ( New human → From MakeHuman ). Asset packs MPFB and MakeHuman II include the base body and a few basic assets. Thousands of extra clothes, hair styles and skins (free, CC0 and CC-BY) can be downloaded from http://www.makehumancommunity.org/ as asset packs and loaded with MPFB → System and resources → Load pack from zip file . See the tutorial A character with MakeHuman . Licences of your characters The program is AGPL-3.0, but characters you export with the standard assets are yours to use, including commercially (the base mesh and standard assets are CC0). Check the licence of extra community assets before you sell work.", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "FreeCAD, Wings 3D, MeshLab and OpenSCAD", "s": "The other 3D modellers on Animux and when each one is the right tool next to Blender.", "u": "software/other-3d.html", "x": "Program Use it when Start FreeCAD 0.20 Props, sets and machines that need exact measurements , or parts for 3D printing. Parametric: change a number and the model updates. freecad OpenSCAD 2021.01 You prefer to describe an object in a few lines of code ( cube , cylinder , difference ). Perfect for repeated parts. openscad Wings 3D 2.2 You want a small, simple polygon modeller for low-poly models and learning box modelling. wings3d MeshLab 2020.09 You need to repair, clean or simplify scans and heavy meshes (photogrammetry, 3D scanners). meshlab Geomview 1.9 You view mathematical surfaces (OFF files). Mostly for teaching. geomview All are in 3D Animation → Modellers . FreeCAD's start page. Moving models into Blender From Export as In Blender FreeCAD File → Export → OBJ or STL (select the object first) File → Import → Wavefront (.obj) OpenSCAD File → Export → STL (render with F6 first) File → Import → STL Wings 3D File → Export → glTF or OBJ File → Import → glTF 2.0 MeshLab File → Export Mesh As → PLY or OBJ File → Import → Stanford PLY Check the scale after importing: CAD programs work in millimetres, Blender in metres. Use the import dialog's Scale (0.001 for mm). OpenSCAD from the command line openscad -o bracket.stl bracket.scad MeshLab. Wings 3D Wings 3D opens with an empty scene. Right-click in the grid to get the menu, for example Cube ; select faces, edges or vertices with the four red cube buttons at the top and right-click again for the tools (Extrude, Bevel, Smooth …). Wings 3D 2.2.9 on Animux. Wings 3D opens no window (r16 only) Fixed in r16.1 ( _25 ): the menu entry and the wings3d command start it safely. On Animux r16 ( _21 and earlier), Wings 3D opens no window on computers without a graphics-card OpenCL driver: the processor OpenCL driver breaks its start-up. Start it from a terminal like this instead: OCL_ICD_VENDORS=/nonexistent wings3d The package animux-menus 1.0.17 fixes the menu entry (Wings 3D then always starts this way); see Guide updates for installing a package file.", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Renderers", "s": "Cycles, EEVEE, POV-Ray, LuxCoreRender, Mitsuba 3, appleseed and pbrt-v4. What each one is good at, how to run its sample scene, and which one to choose.", "u": "software/renderers.html", "x": "A renderer calculates the final picture from a 3D scene: how light bounces, how glass bends it, how shadows fall. Animux has seven, from very easy to research-grade. Which one should I use? Renderer Choose it when How you use it Cycles (in Blender) Almost always: realistic, fast enough, in the program you model in Blender → Render Engine: Cycles EEVEE (in Blender) Fast previews, cartoons, motion graphics Blender → Render Engine: EEVEE POV-Ray 3.7 You like describing scenes in text; classic CG, maths art, teaching menu Renderers → POV-Ray , or povray LuxCoreRender 2.11 Very realistic light: caustics, glass, interiors Python scripts ( animux-vfx-python ) Mitsuba 3 3.9 Research, spectral rendering, inverse rendering Python or mitsuba scene.xml appleseed 2.1 beta Production-style physically based rendering of .appleseed projects menu Renderers → appleseed , or appleseed.cli pbrt-v4 Learning how renderers work, with the book Physically Based Rendering pbrt scene.pbrt All seven side by side, with full names and what each does best: Renderer comparison . Beginners: use Cycles , explained in Blender and the first-render tutorial . Processor features Cycles (Blender 5) and appleseed need SSE4.2; Mitsuba and LuxCore also need AVX. See Requirements . Try every renderer in two minutes Each renderer has a sample scene in /usr/share/animux/samples . The self-test renders all of them and saves the pictures in ~/animux-selftest/<date>/ : animux-selftest povray blender mitsuba luxcore appleseed pbrt gaffer These are the pictures the self-test made on Animux (in a virtual machine, a few seconds each): Cycles (Blender 5.2): samples/blender/turntable.py POV-Ray: samples/povray/animux-spheres.pov LuxCoreRender: samples/luxcore/render.py Mitsuba 3: samples/mitsuba/render.py appleseed: the helloworld example pbrt-v4: samples/pbrt/animux-spheres.pbrt Gaffer with Cycles: samples/gaffer/render.py Synfig: frame 12 of samples/synfig/bouncing-ball.sif POV-Ray POV-Ray reads a text file that describes the scene, and writes a picture. #include \"colors.inc\" camera { location <0, 2, -6> look_at <0, 1, 0> } light_source { <-5, 10, -10> color White } plane { y, 0 pigment { checker Gray White } } sphere { <0, 1, 0>, 1 pigment { color Red } finish { phong 0.8 reflection 0.2 } } Save that as ball.pov , then either choose 3D Animation → Renderers → POV-Ray and pick the file (the picture opens by itself and is saved in ~/Pictures/povray/ ), or type: povray +Iball.pov +Oball.png +W1280 +H720 +A -D +A turns on anti-aliasing (smooth edges), -D renders without a preview window. Hundreds of example scenes are in /usr/share/povray-3.7/scenes . POV-Ray asks for a scene file; the sample scenes folder opens first. LuxCoreRender LuxCore is used here as a Python module in Animux's ready-made Python, animux-vfx-python : animux-vfx-python /usr/share/animux/samples/luxcore/render.py ~/luxcore.png Read render.py to see how a scene is described (camera, materials, lights) and how long it renders (a time or quality limit). LuxCore's Blender add-on ( BlendLuxCore ) is not included in r16. Info On Animux LuxCore renders on the processor . It ignores the software OpenCL drivers that come with Debian, because they made it crash. On a computer with a real OpenCL GPU driver you can let it use the GPU by starting it with ANIMUX_KEEP_ALL_OPENCL=1 . Mitsuba 3 mitsuba scene.xml -o out.exr animux-vfx-python /usr/share/animux/samples/mitsuba/render.py ~/mitsuba.exr Mitsuba writes EXR images; view them with DJV or convert them: oiiotool ~/mitsuba.exr -o ~/mitsuba.png . Mitsuba 3 can render in RGB or spectral mode, and can compute how the picture changes when the scene changes (for research and machine learning). Documentation: https://mitsuba.readthedocs.io/ . appleseed Only the command-line renderer is included (the appleseed.studio editor needs Python 2.7, which Debian 12 no longer has). The menu entry asks for a .appleseed project, renders it and shows the result. appleseed.cli \"/opt/animux/ap", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Renderer comparison", "s": "The seven renderers in Animux side by side: full name, who makes it, how it calculates light, what it runs on, what it does best and when to use it.", "u": "software/renderer-comparison.html", "x": "A renderer turns a 3D scene into a picture. They differ in how they calculate light : following rays of light like a camera does ( path tracing ), drawing the scene the way games do ( rasterisation ), or following light from both the camera and the lamps ( bidirectional ). That decides what each one is good at. Animux installs all seven, ready to use. Full name Made by How it calculates light Runs on Best at Use it for Cycles Cycles Render Engine Blender Foundation, in Blender Path tracing, physically based Processor or graphics card Realistic light, soft shadows, glass, built-in denoising Final frames of most films; also renders from Gaffer EEVEE Extra Easy Virtual Environment Engine Blender Foundation, in Blender Rasterisation (real time) with ray-traced reflections Graphics card Instant feedback, stylised and cartoon looks Previews, motion graphics, stylised films ( Flow was rendered in EEVEE) POV-Ray Persistence of Vision Raytracer POV-Ray team, since 1991 Ray tracing from a scene written as text Processor Exact shapes from maths, scenes as code Teaching, maths art, generated scenes LuxCoreRender LuxCoreRender LuxCoreRender project Unbiased path tracing and bidirectional path tracing Processor (GPU with a driver) Caustics, light through glass and water, interiors Product shots, architecture, the most accurate light appleseed appleseed appleseed project Physically based path tracing, OSL shaders Processor Production-style scenes and shading networks Studying a VFX production renderer Mitsuba 3 Mitsuba 3 Realistic Graphics Lab, EPFL Spectral and polarised path tracing; differentiable Processor Light by wavelength; works backwards from a picture to the scene Research, scientific images, inverse rendering pbrt-v4 Physically Based Rendering, version 4 Pharr, Jakob and Humphreys Spectral path tracing, explained line by line in a book Processor Every line of code explained in the book Learning how renderers work Which one, when? You want… Take A finished, realistic frame from Blender Cycles To see the light while you work, or a stylised look EEVEE One light rig for many shots, studio-style Gaffer with Cycles ( Gaffer ) Caustics and glass that must be physically right LuxCoreRender To describe a scene in code POV-Ray To research or learn rendering Mitsuba 3 , pbrt-v4 Shaders: OSL OSL (Open Shading Language) describes materials as small programs. It was developed at Sony Pictures Imageworks and is the shader language of film rendering: Cycles, appleseed and Gaffer read OSL, so one material works in all three. Animux includes the OSL compiler oslc ( Pipeline formats ). How to start each renderer, sample scenes and render times: Renderers .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Flamenco render farm", "s": "Let several computers render one Blender animation together, using Flamenco Manager, Workers and shared storage, and the Animux render farm boot roles.", "u": "software/flamenco.html", "x": "Flamenco Menu 3D Animation → Render Queue Managers → Flamenco Manager Version 3.9.3 Command animux-flamenco-manager Good for Intermediate Files Blender jobs Licence GPL-3.0 Website flamenco.blender.org Self-test animux-selftest flamenco A render farm is a group of computers that share the rendering of an animation: each computer renders different frames. Flamenco is Blender's official, free render farm software. Part Runs on Job Manager one computer (it can also render) keeps the list of jobs and hands out frames; has a web page to watch progress Worker every computer that renders asks the Manager for work, runs Blender, reports back Blender add-on the artist's computer sends a .blend file to the Manager as a job Shared storage a network folder all computers must see the project files and write the frames, at the same path 1. Shared storage Every computer must reach the project at the same place, for example /mnt/farm . The simplest way on an all-Animux farm is NFS from the Manager computer. On the Manager, first create the folder: sudo mkdir -p /srv/farm Only if the Manager runs from the USB stick (live session): the live system disk lives in memory, and NFS cannot share folders on it. Put the folder on a real disk or USB drive (mount it on /srv/farm ), or, for a test, on a RAM disk of 8 GB: sudo mount -t tmpfs -o size=8G tmpfs /srv/farm (A RAM disk uses memory and is emptied when the Manager switches off, so copy the finished frames away.) Then share it (change 192.168.1.0/24 to your own network): sudo chown $USER /srv/farm echo '/srv/farm 192.168.1.0/24(rw,sync,no_subtree_check,fsid=0)' | sudo tee -a /etc/exports sudo systemctl unmask nfs-kernel-server && sudo systemctl enable --now nfs-kernel-server sudo exportfs -ra && sudo exportfs -v sudo ln -s /srv/farm /mnt/farm sudo exportfs -v must list /srv/farm . fsid=0 makes /srv/farm the top of the share, so the Workers mount manager-pc:/ (this also works in a live session, where the plain path does not). On each Worker (replace manager-pc with the Manager's name or IP address): sudo mkdir -p /mnt/farm sudo mount -t nfs manager-pc:/ /mnt/farm A Windows share or NAS works too, as long as every computer uses the same path. 2. Start the Manager On the Manager computer: 3D Animation → Render Queue Managers → Flamenco Manager . A terminal window opens (keep it open; closing it stops the Manager) and the browser shows the setup page at http://localhost:8080/ : Flamenco Manager's first-time setup in the browser. The setup asks for: Shared storage : /mnt/farm . Blender : choose Blender found on $PATH ( /usr/bin/blender , Blender 5.2.3). Settings and the job database are kept in ~/flamenco . Without a desktop (text login), start the Manager with the same command the menu uses: animux-flamenco-manager , and open http://manager-pc:8080/ from another computer. Only Animux r16 ( _21 ): Workers get no work Fixed in r16.1 ( _25 ). On r16 the Manager accepts jobs, but no Worker ever starts them: the Manager tries to keep its task logs in /opt/animux/flamenco/flamenco-manager-storage , where it may not write (the web page shows the tasks as queued forever). Fix it once, after the setup page, in a terminal on the Manager: sed -i \"s#^local_manager_storage_path:.*#local_manager_storage_path: $HOME/flamenco/flamenco-manager-storage#\" ~/flamenco/flamenco-manager.yaml then close the Manager window and start it again. The package animux-flamenco 3.9.3-animux2 does this by itself. 3. Start the Workers On every render computer (including the Manager, if it should render too), open a terminal: flamenco-worker Workers find the Manager on the local network by themselves. If not, tell them where it is: flamenco-worker -manager http://manager-pc:8080/ The Manager's web page lists them under Workers . 4. Send a job from Blender On the artist's computer open http://manager-pc:8080/ in Firefox and download the Blender add-on from the Manager's web page (the link on the start page). In Blender: Edit → Preferences", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Gaffer", "s": "The node-based lighting and look-development tool used by film studios, included with Cortex, OSL, OpenUSD and Alembic, and rendering with Cycles.", "u": "software/gaffer.html", "x": "Gaffer Menu 3D Animation → Pipeline Tools → Gaffer Version 1.7.5.0 Command gaffer Good for Advanced Files .gfr; reads USD, Alembic, SceneCache Licence BSD-3-Clause Website gafferhq.org Self-test animux-selftest gaffer Gaffer is a free tool from Image Engine, a visual-effects studio, for the work after modelling and animation: assembling scenes, lighting, look development (materials) and sending renders . Everything is a node graph : boxes connected by lines, each doing one step. Change an early node and everything after it updates. This makes it possible to light hundreds of shots the same way. Is Gaffer for me? For a personal or school film, Blender alone is enough . Gaffer is worth learning if you want to work in a VFX or animation studio pipeline, or your project has many shots that share lighting. Gaffer: the viewer at the top, the node graph at the bottom left, node settings on the right. Start 3D Animation → Pipeline Tools → Gaffer , or gaffer . Gaffer's window has: Viewer : the 3D scene (or rendered image) produced by the selected node; Graph Editor : the nodes; press Tab in it to add a node by name; Node Editor : settings of the selected node; Hierarchy / Scene Inspector : the objects in the scene at that point of the graph. A first graph Add nodes with Tab in the Graph Editor and connect an output (bottom of a node) to an input (top) by dragging. The sample scene uses this chain: Sphere , Plane , Camera and two CyclesLight nodes (one background_light for the sky, one distant_light for the sun) → all into one Group . CyclesShader ( principled_bsdf , pick a base colour) + PathFilter (path /group/sphere ) → ShaderAssignment : gives the sphere its material. StandardOptions : switch on Render Camera ( /group/camera ) and Resolution . CyclesOptions : samples and other Cycles settings. Outputs : where the image is written (add an Interactive output for previews, a Batch output for files). InteractiveRender (live preview in the Viewer) or Render (writes the files), renderer Cycles . The sample /usr/share/animux/samples/gaffer/render.py builds such a graph by script and renders it: gaffer env python /usr/share/animux/samples/gaffer/render.py ~/gaffer.exr Bringing in scenes SceneReader nodes load USD ( .usd , .usda , .usdc ) and Alembic ( .abc ) files exported from Blender ( File → Export → Universal Scene Description or Alembic ). Lighting stays in Gaffer; when the animation changes, re-export and Gaffer picks it up. What comes with it Gaffer on Animux includes the libraries studios use, with command-line tools you can run directly: Library Tools See Cortex Python libraries for VFX pipelines ( import IECore inside gaffer python ) https://github.com/ImageEngine/cortex OpenUSD usdview , usdcat , usdchecker , usdtree , usddiff , usdrecord , usdzip , usdresolve , usdedit , usdstitch Pipeline formats OSL oslc , oslinfo , testshade Pipeline formats Alembic abcls , abctree , abcecho , abcechobounds , abcconvert , abcstitcher Pipeline formats Cycles renders from Gaffer OpenColorIO colour management Pipeline formats Command line and scripting gaffer execute -script lighting.gfr -nodes CyclesRender -frames 1-100 gaffer python myscript.py Learn more: https://www.gafferhq.org/documentation/ and Gaffer's Help menu (examples and tutorials are built in).", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Pipeline formats and tools", "s": "OpenUSD, Alembic, OpenEXR, OpenColorIO, OpenVDB, OSL and MaterialX, what each studio format is for and the tools Animux has for it.", "u": "software/pipeline-formats.html", "x": "Studios pass work between many programs. These open formats, most of them from the Academy Software Foundation , make that possible. Blender reads and writes most of them; Animux adds the command-line tools to check and convert them. Who made each format and why, in one card each: Studio formats . Format Holds File Blender OpenUSD whole scenes: objects, materials, lights, cameras, layers .usd .usda .usdc .usdz File → Import/Export → USD Alembic baked moving geometry (animation caches) .abc File → Import/Export → Alembic OpenEXR high-quality images, many layers (passes) .exr Output file format OpenEXR OpenColorIO colour spaces and looks config.ocio Blender uses OCIO internally OpenVDB volumes: smoke, fire, clouds .vdb Add → Volume → Import OpenVDB OSL custom materials as code .osl Cycles → Script node (CPU) MaterialX materials shared between programs .mtlx via USD export OpenUSD Universal Scene Description, made by Pixar, is the scene format of modern pipelines (Blender, Houdini, Maya, Omniverse, Unreal). usdview /usr/share/animux/samples/usd/animux-scene.usda # look at a scene usdcat scene.usda -o scene.usdc # text → binary usdcat scene.usdc # print a binary file as text usdtree scene.usda # list what is inside usdchecker scene.usda # check for errors usdzip -a scene.usdz scene.usda # package for AR (iPhone) usdview showing the Animux sample scene. Python: animux-vfx-python -c \"from pxr import Usd; print(Usd.Stage.Open('scene.usda').GetRootLayer().ExportToString())\" . Alembic abcls -r /usr/share/animux/samples/alembic/animux-cube.abc # list objects abctree file.abc # show the hierarchy abcecho file.abc # print details The sample is a cube spinning over 24 frames. Import it in Blender with File → Import → Alembic . OpenEXR and OpenImageIO EXR keeps real light values (brighter than white) and several passes in one file: diffuse, specular, depth, object IDs. Render to EXR, then grade and comp. exrheader render.exr # what is inside (channels, compression) oiiotool render.exr -o render.png # convert oiiotool render.exr --resize 50% -o half.exr oiiotool a.exr b.exr --over -o comp.exr # put a over b iinfo -v render.exr View EXR files with DJV . OpenColorIO OCIO describes colour spaces (sRGB, ACEScg, Filmic/AgX…) so every program shows the same colours. ociocheck --iconfig /usr/share/animux/samples/ocio/config.ocio Point programs to a config with the OCIO environment variable: OCIO=/path/config.ocio krita . OpenVDB vdb_print -l smoke.vdb Blender writes VDB files from smoke simulations ( Physics → Fluid → Cache → Format: OpenVDB ). OSL (Open Shading Language) oslc stripes.osl # compile to stripes.oso oslinfo stripes.oso # list its parameters In Blender: Cycles, Device: CPU , tick Open Shading Language in Render properties, then add a Script node in the shader editor. Animux VFX Python animux-vfx-python is a Python 3.11 with all these libraries installed together, so scripts work without setup: Module Import as OpenUSD (usd-core 26.11) from pxr import Usd, UsdGeom OpenImageIO 3.2 import OpenImageIO as oiio OpenEXR 3.5 import OpenEXR OpenColorIO 2.6 import PyOpenColorIO as ocio MaterialX 1.39 import MaterialX Mitsuba 3.9 import mitsuba as mi LuxCoreRender 2.11 import pyluxcore NumPy 2.4 import numpy It lives in /opt/animux/vfx-env and is managed by the animux-vfx-python package; do not install into it. For extra libraries make your own environment on top of it: /opt/animux/vfx-env/bin/python -m venv --system-site-packages ~/myenv , then ~/myenv/bin/pip install name .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Studio formats", "s": "OpenUSD, Alembic, OpenEXR, OpenColorIO and OpenVDB, one card each: which studio made it, what problem it solves, and what it looks like in Animux.", "u": "software/studio-formats.html", "x": "A film passes through many programs: modelling, animation, effects, lighting, compositing. Each step must read what the step before wrote, without losing anything. The big studios solved this with their own file formats and then gave them away as open source . Today most of them are kept by the Academy Software Foundation (ASWF), part of the Linux Foundation. Animux installs them with their tools. Pixar · open source since 2016 OpenUSD Universal Scene Description · .usd .usda .usdc · viewer: usdview The problem: on one shot, modelling, layout, animation and lighting all want to work at the same time. The answer: a scene made of layers . Each department writes its own layer on top of the others, and a stronger layer wins. Nobody overwrites anybody's file. Pixar built USD for its own films and released it in 2016. It is now read by Blender, Houdini, Maya, Unreal and NVIDIA Omniverse. Source: Pixar, \"Open Source Announcement\", openusd.org Sony Pictures Imageworks and ILM · 2010 Alembic Baked animation cache · .abc · tools: abcls , abctree The problem: a rig only works in the program it was built in. The answer: the finished movement is baked : every vertex position on every frame is stored. The cache plays the same in Blender, Gaffer, Houdini or Maya, with no rig needed. Imageworks and Industrial Light & Magic announced it together at SIGGRAPH 2010. Source: Alembic project, alembic.io Industrial Light & Magic · open source since 2003 OpenEXR High dynamic range images · .exr · viewer: DJV The problem: a PNG cuts off light: the sun and a lamp both become plain white. The answer: EXR keeps the real brightness of every pixel, so exposure can be changed later, and stores passes (light, shadow, reflections, depth) as separate layers in one file for compositing. Source: OpenEXR project, openexr.com Sony Pictures Imageworks OpenColorIO Colour management · config.ocio · ACES The problem: the same frame looks different in the 3D program, the compositor and the editor. The answer: one colour configuration shared by every program, so a red stays the same red from render to final grade. Blender, Krita, Gaffer and DaVinci Resolve all use it. Source: OpenColorIO project, opencolorio.org DreamWorks Animation · 2012 OpenVDB Volumes · .vdb · smoke, fire, clouds The problem: smoke and clouds are mostly empty air, and storing every point of a box wastes memory. The answer: a sparse grid that stores only the cells where something is. Blender writes its smoke simulations as VDB, and renderers read them. Source: OpenVDB project, openvdb.org Commands and examples for each format: Pipeline formats .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Synfig Studio", "s": "2D vector animation with automatic in-between frames and bone rigs. Ideal for cut-out characters, motion graphics and explainer films.", "u": "software/synfig.html", "x": "Synfig Studio Menu 2D Animation → Synfig Studio Version 1.5.1 Command synfigstudio Good for Beginner Files .sifz, .sif; exports PNG sequences, GIF, video Licence GPL-3.0 Website synfig.org Self-test animux-selftest synfig In Synfig you do not draw every frame. You set the important poses ( waypoints ) and Synfig calculates the frames in between ( tweening ). Characters are built from shapes and can be moved with bones , like a puppet. Synfig Studio with the Animux bouncing-ball sample: canvas in the middle, tools on the left, parameters and layers on the right, timetrack at the bottom. The window Panel Use Toolbox (left) transform, circle, rectangle, spline (draw shapes), fill, text, bones… Canvas (middle) your picture; the Animation mode button at its bottom right (red when on) records changes as waypoints Params settings of the selected layer (colour, position, radius…) Layers every shape is a layer; group them to build characters Timetrack waypoints over time; drag them to change timing Animate a ball in five steps The sample /usr/share/animux/samples/synfig/bouncing-ball.sif is a finished example; open it with File → Open to look at it. To make your own: File → New . Set the length in Canvas → Properties → Time (for example End time 48 at 24 fps = 2 seconds). Choose the Circle tool and drag on the canvas to draw a ball. Click the Animation mode button (bottom right of the canvas): the canvas gets a red frame. At frame 0, place the ball at the top. Move the time slider to frame 12 and drag the ball to the bottom; at frame 24 back to the top. Synfig adds waypoints and fills in the frames. Press Play . Turn animation mode off when you are done. Make it look natural: right-click a waypoint → Both → Ease In/Out for slow-in and slow-out. Export File → Render : choose the file name and Target : png (image sequence, best quality, for editing in Kdenlive), gif for the web, ffmpeg for a video file. Or from a terminal: synfig bouncing-ball.sif -t png -o ~/ball/frame.png # all frames synfig bouncing-ball.sif -t gif -o ~/ball.gif synfig bouncing-ball.sif -t png --time 12 -o ~/frame12.png # one frame Learn more Synfig's manual and tutorials: https://synfig.readthedocs.io/ Tutorial on this guide: A bouncing ball in Synfig", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "OpenToonz", "s": "The studio 2D animation program: hand-drawn and cut-out animation with the Xsheet, scan cleanup, ink and paint, and node-based effects. New in r16.3.", "u": "software/opentoonz.html", "x": "OpenToonz Menu 2D Animation → OpenToonz Version 1.8.0 (built by Animux, new in r16.3) Command opentoonz Good for Intermediate Files .tnz scenes with .tlv, .pli and raster levels; renders PNG/TIFF sequences and video Licence BSD-3-Clause Website opentoonz.github.io Self-test animux-selftest opentoonz OpenToonz is the open-source version of Toonz , the 2D animation software used in studios, among them Studio Ghibli, whose edition it is based on. It covers the whole traditional 2D pipeline: draw or scan the drawings, clean up the lines, paint them, time them on the Xsheet , add effects and render. New in r16.3 OpenToonz is on Animux from release r16.3 . Debian has no OpenToonz package, so Animux builds version 1.8.0 from its source code (package animux-opentoonz , installed in /opt/animux/opentoonz ). Start it 2D Animation → OpenToonz , or type opentoonz . The first start copies OpenToonz's brushes, templates and settings to ~/.config/OpenToonz ; that takes a few seconds. The Startup window opens on top: Keep the project sandbox (OpenToonz's practice project), or click New Project... for a real one. Click Create a New Scene , type a Scene Name and click Create Scene . Untick Show this at startup if you do not want this window every time. The main parts Part What it is Rooms (tabs top right) ready-made window layouts: Drawing , Timeline , Cleanup , InknPaint , Xsheet , … Viewer the canvas; the red frame is the camera Xsheet / Timeline columns are layers, rows are frames: which drawing is shown on which frame Level Strip all drawings of the current level FX Schematic effects as connected nodes ( Windows → Schematic ) A level is a set of drawings (one character, one prop). OpenToonz has three kinds: Toonz Raster ( .tlv , pencil-like lines with separate ink and paint), Toonz Vector ( .pli , editable lines) and plain Raster ( .tif , .png ). A first drawing In the Drawing room, click the cell of frame 1 in column Col1 . Press B for the Brush and draw in the viewer. OpenToonz makes a new level for it. Click frame 2 and draw the next pose. Onion skin ( View → Onion Skin ) shows the frame before. File → Save All ( Ctrl+S ). The scene goes to scenes/ , the drawings to drawings/ of the project. Render → Output Settings to choose the format and folder, then Render → Render ( Ctrl+Shift+R ). Files go to the project's outputs/ folder. A ready-made sample project is in /usr/share/animux/samples/opentoonz/animux-lines ; animux-selftest opentoonz renders it. Render without the window OpenToonz renders with tcomposer . After you have started OpenToonz once: LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/animux/opentoonz/lib/opentoonz \\ /opt/animux/opentoonz/bin/tcomposer ~/.config/OpenToonz/stuff/sandbox/scenes/myscene.tnz -o /tmp/out.png This writes /tmp/out.0001.png , /tmp/out.0002.png , … one file per frame. On a server without a screen, put QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen in front. When to use something else Simple frame-by-frame drawing, for beginners and schools: Pencil2D . Animation by moving shapes and bones with automatic in-betweens: Synfig . Painterly animation with brushes and textures: Krita . Help: the OpenToonz manual at opentoonz.readthedocs.io .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Pencil2D and TupiTube", "s": "Hand-drawn, frame-by-frame animation the traditional way, with onion skin, a timeline and export to video or GIF.", "u": "software/pencil2d.html", "x": "Pencil2D Menu 2D Animation → Pencil2D Version 0.6.6 Command pencil2d Good for Beginner Files .pclx; exports PNG sequence, GIF, MP4 Licence GPL-2.0 Website pencil2d.org Pencil2D works like a flip book: you draw a picture on each frame and play them in a row. Onion skin shows the previous and next drawings faintly, so you can draw the in-betweens. It is simple enough for children and good enough for professional rough animation. Pencil2D: tools on the left, the canvas in the middle, colours on the right, the timeline with layers at the bottom. Layer types Layer For Bitmap pencil and brush drawing, like paper Vector clean lines you can still edit; fill areas with the bucket Camera pan and zoom over the drawing Sound dialogue or music to time your animation A first animation See the full tutorial: Hand-drawn animation in Pencil2D . In short: Draw on frame 1. Click frame 3 in the timeline and click + ( Add Frame ); draw the next pose. Switch on onion skin (the onion buttons above the timeline) to see the frame before. Press Play . Change the speed with the FPS box (12 fps is common for hand-drawn animation, \"on twos\" at 24 fps). File → Export → Movie for MP4 or GIF, or Image Sequence for PNG files. Pencil2D saves projects as .pclx . TupiTube TupiTube (Debian package tupi ) is another beginner-friendly 2D animation program, made for schools: draw, tween objects between positions, and export. Start it from 2D Animation → TupiTube or tupi . TupiTube. When to use something else Painting-style animation with brushes and textures: Krita . Animation by moving shapes and bones instead of drawing every frame: Synfig . 2D drawing combined with 3D: Grease Pencil in Blender .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Krita", "s": "Professional digital painting with hundreds of brushes, layers, a drawing-tablet-friendly interface and a full frame-by-frame animation timeline.", "u": "software/krita.html", "x": "Krita Menu 2D Graphics → Bitmap Editors → Krita Version 5.3.4.1 Command krita Good for Beginner to professional Files .kra; PSD, PNG, JPEG, EXR, TIFF; renders animation to video Licence GPL-3.0 Website krita.org Krita is made by artists for painting : concept art, character design, backgrounds, textures, comics and storyboards. It also has a complete animation workspace for hand-drawn animation with brushes. Krita's welcome screen. Start a new file on the left; recent files are in the middle. Start painting File → New → choose a size (for film: 1920 × 1080 , 300 dpi for print) → Create . Pick a brush in Brush Presets (right), a colour in the Advanced Color Selector . Paint. [ ] change brush size, E switches to eraser mode, Ctrl+Z undoes. Add layers with Insert , or the + in the Layers panel. Keep sketch, line art and colour on separate layers. File → Save as .kra (keeps layers); File → Export to PNG or JPEG. Animation Window → Workspace → Animation . The Timeline and Animation panels appear. Draw on frame 0. In the timeline, click the next frame, right-click → Create Blank Frame , and draw. Onion Skins panel: see the frames before and after. Set the frame rate and start/end in the Animation panel; press Play . File → Render Animation : choose Video (MP4, uses FFmpeg) or Image Sequence . Drawing tablets Pressure works out of the box. Tune it in Settings → Configure Krita → Tablet settings (pressure curve). If the cursor is offset on a multi-monitor setup, set the tablet's area in Krita's tablet settings. Colour management Settings → Configure Krita → Color Management selects the screen profile and OCIO configuration; important when you paint textures or matte paintings for 3D. Learn more Krita manual: https://docs.krita.org/ Tutorial on this guide: Paint an animation in Krita", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Stopmotion", "s": "Capture stop-motion animation with a webcam or import photos from a camera, check each pose with onion skin, and export a film.", "u": "software/stopmotion.html", "x": "Stopmotion (Linux Stopmotion) Menu StopMotion Animation → Stopmotion Version 0.8.6 Command stopmotion Good for Beginner Files .dat project; image folders; exports video Licence GPL-2.0 Website linuxstopmotion.org Stop motion is animation with real objects: clay figures, LEGO, paper cut-outs, toys. You move the object a tiny bit, take a photo, move it again, take another. Stopmotion helps you take the photos and line up each new pose with the previous one. Stopmotion: the frame list on the left, the camera view in the middle. What you need A webcam (any USB webcam that works on Linux), or a camera whose photos you copy in afterwards. A tripod or something that holds the camera completely still. Steady light : close the curtains and use lamps. Daylight changes and makes the film flicker. Capturing with a webcam Plug in the webcam and start StopMotion Animation → Stopmotion . Settings → Preferences → Video Import : choose the device ( /dev/video0 is usually the first webcam). Click the camera button to start the live view. Turn on onion skinning (mix slider: 1 or 2 previous frames). The last photo shows faintly over the live image. Move your figure a little, then press Space (or the capture button) to take a frame. Repeat. Press Play to check the movement. Twelve frames per second is a good start; 24 is smoother but needs twice as many photos. Photos from a camera Edit → Add frames (or drag image files into the frame list) to add photos taken with a camera or phone. Rename them in order first (for example 0001.jpg , 0002.jpg ). Sound and export Add sound with the Sound button of a scene. File → Export → Video writes a video file (it uses FFmpeg); File → Export → Cinelerra sends the frames to Cinelerra. Or export the frames and put them together with FFmpeg: ffmpeg -framerate 12 -i %04d.jpg -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -crf 18 film.mp4 In a virtual machine The VM must pass the webcam through: in VirtualBox install the Extension Pack and use Devices → Webcams . See VirtualBox . Tutorial: Stop motion with a webcam .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "GIMP, Inkscape and other image tools", "s": "Photo editing with GIMP, vector drawing with Inkscape, sketching with MyPaint, pixel art with GrafX2 and mtPaint, and batch work with ImageMagick.", "u": "software/gimp-inkscape.html", "x": "Program Best for Start GIMP 3.2 fixing and retouching renders and photos, textures, cut-outs, batch editing gimp Inkscape 1.4 logos, titles, clean character designs, backgrounds: shapes that stay sharp at any size (SVG) inkscape MyPaint 2.0 fast sketching and concept painting on an endless canvas mypaint GrafX2 2.8 pixel art and sprites with palettes grafx2 mtPaint 3.40 very quick pixel edits mtpaint ImageMagick changing hundreds of images at once from the terminal convert , mogrify GIMP GIMP with the render from the first tutorial. Common jobs: Adjust a render : Colors → Curves or Levels ; Colors → Hue-Saturation . Cut out a background : Tools → Selection Tools → Foreground Select , then Layer → Transparency → Add Alpha Channel and delete. Make a texture tile seamlessly : Filters → Map → Tile Seamless . Resize : Image → Scale Image . Export : File → Export As (GIMP's own format is .xcf ; Export writes PNG/JPEG). Non-destructive filters (new in GIMP 3): filters stay editable on the layer; click the fx icon in the Layers panel to change or remove them. G'MIC (hundreds of extra effects: painterly looks, denoise, cartoon) is not available for GIMP 3.2 on Animux yet. The previous GIMP 2.10 with G'MIC is still installed: type gimp.debian in a terminal and use Filters → G'MIC-Qt there. GIMP 3.2, Krita 5.3 and Inkscape 1.4 Animux r16.1 ( _25 ) includes them; Debian's Krita 5.1 and Inkscape 1.2 are no longer on the ISO, and GIMP 2.10 stays as gimp.debian for G'MIC. Animux r16 ( _21 ) shipped only GIMP 2.10, Krita 5.1 and Inkscape 1.2 from Debian 12; there, the packages animux-gimp , animux-krita and animux-inkscape add the official current versions (the menu entries and the commands gimp , krita and inkscape then start the new versions). Install them like a guide update (package files or, once online, sudo apt install animux-gimp animux-krita animux-inkscape ). AI assistants in GIMP, Krita and Inkscape An AI assistant can work in the new GIMP, Krita and Inkscape through MCP: animux-mcp gimp on , animux-mcp krita on , animux-mcp inkscape on . See AI agents . Inkscape Inkscape. Draw with the rectangle, ellipse, star and Bézier pen tools; colour with Object → Fill and Stroke (Ctrl+Shift+F). Text for titles: T tool; convert to shapes with Path → Object to Path before sharing the file. Export PNG at any size: File → Export (Ctrl+Shift+E). Inkscape files (SVG) also open in Blender ( File → Import → SVG ) and Synfig. From the terminal: inkscape title.svg --export-type=png --export-width=1920 . GIMP also works without a window, for scripts and AI agents, with Python (GIMP 3 API): gimp-console -i --batch-interpreter=python-fu-eval -b \"exec(open('my_script.py').read())\" --quit MyPaint MyPaint. Press Tab to hide the panels and paint full screen. The canvas grows as you paint. Save as .ora (OpenRaster, opens in Krita and GIMP). ImageMagick: many images at once mogrify -resize 50% *.png # make every PNG half size (overwrites!) mogrify -format jpg *.png # convert all PNG to JPEG convert in.png -gravity south -pointsize 48 -annotate +0+20 \"Shot 10\" out.png montage frame_*.png -tile 6x -geometry 320x180+4+4 contact-sheet.png Work on a copy of your files: mogrify changes them in place.", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Kdenlive", "s": "The recommended video editor on Animux. Put shots and sound on a timeline, add titles and transitions, and export an MP4 for YouTube or a festival.", "u": "software/kdenlive.html", "x": "Kdenlive Menu NLV Editors → Kdenlive Version 22.12.3 Command kdenlive Good for Beginner Files .kdenlive projects; almost every video, audio and image format Licence GPL-3.0 Website kdenlive.org When your shots are rendered, a video editor puts them together with dialogue, music and titles. Kdenlive is powerful and still easy to start with. Kdenlive with the turntable animation in the project bin and on the timeline. The window Area Use Project Bin (top left) all your clips: videos, image sequences, sound, titles Clip Monitor / Project Monitor (top right) play the selected clip / the whole edit Effects, Transitions (tabs) drag them onto clips Timeline (bottom) video tracks (V1, V2…) and audio tracks (A1, A2…) Edit a film in six steps Project → Project Settings : choose the format of your film, for example HD 1080p 24 fps . Do this first. Add clips : right-click in the Project Bin → Add Clip or Folder (or drag files from the file manager). For a numbered image sequence ( 0001.png , 0002.png …), tick Import image sequence in the file dialog. Drag clips to the timeline in order. Trim a clip by dragging its ends; cut at the playhead with Shift+R . Sound : drag music and dialogue onto the audio tracks. Change volume with the Volume effect or the clip's line. Titles : right-click the Bin → Add Title Clip . Transitions : overlap two clips on different tracks and add a Dissolve ( Wipe , …). Export : Project → Render (Ctrl+Enter) → preset MP4-H264/AAC → choose the file name → Render to File . Big projects run slowly? Turn on proxy clips ( Project Settings → Proxy ). Kdenlive edits small copies and uses the originals only for the final render. Good export settings For Settings YouTube / Vimeo MP4-H264/AAC, same resolution and frame rate as the project, quality 18–23 Festival / archive ProRes or DNxHR (in the render presets) or a PNG image sequence + WAV Phone / messaging MP4-H264, 1280×720 Kdenlive also renders from the command line using the melt program it is built on. Learn more: https://docs.kdenlive.org/ · Tutorial: Edit your film in Kdenlive .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "More video tools", "s": "OpenShot, Cinelerra-GG and Avidemux for editing, HandBrake and FFmpeg for converting, DJV for reviewing renders, VLC for playing, and screen recording.", "u": "software/video-tools.html", "x": "Program Use it to Start Kdenlive edit a film (recommended) kdenlive OpenShot 2.6 edit very simply: drag, drop, export openshot-qt Cinelerra-GG edit with professional colour grading, compositing, motion tracking cin Avidemux 2.8 cut a single file quickly, filter, re-encode avidemux HandBrake 1.6 convert a finished film with ready presets ghb FFmpeg 5.1 convert anything from the terminal; image sequence → video ffmpeg DJV 3.7 play image sequences and EXR renders at true speed, compare versions djv VLC 3.0 play every video and sound file vlc MKVToolNix combine video, audio tracks and subtitles in one MKV mkvtoolnix-gui Subtitle Editor , Gnome Subtitles write and time subtitles subtitleeditor OpenShot OpenShot. Import files (Ctrl+F), drag them to a track, press Export Video (the red button). Good for school projects. Cinelerra-GG Cinelerra-GG has several windows: program, viewer, compositor and resources. Cinelerra is very capable but different from other editors (several windows, many options). The manual is at https://www.cinelerra-gg.org/download/CinelerraGG_Manual.pdf . Avidemux Avidemux with the turntable video. Open a video, set A and B markers to select a part, choose Video Output (for example Mpeg4 AVC (x264) ) or Copy to keep quality, and File → Save . Copy mode cuts without re-encoding, in seconds. HandBrake HandBrake. Open Source → choose your film → pick a Preset (for example General → Fast 1080p30 ) → Start Encode . DJV: review renders and image sequences DJV showing a render. Studios check renders with a player like DJV: it plays image sequences ( render_0001.exr …) at the correct frame rate, shows EXR channels and exposure, and compares two versions side by side ( Compare menu). File → Open and choose the first frame; DJV finds the rest of the sequence. FFmpeg: the five most useful commands # PNG image sequence → MP4 video (24 frames per second) ffmpeg -framerate 24 -i render_%04d.png -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -crf 18 film.mp4 # add a soundtrack (stops at the shorter of the two) ffmpeg -i film.mp4 -i music.wav -c:v copy -c:a aac -shortest film-with-sound.mp4 # make a GIF for the web (480 px wide) ffmpeg -i film.mp4 -vf \"fps=12,scale=480:-1:flags=lanczos\" film.gif # cut 10 seconds starting at 0:05, without re-encoding ffmpeg -ss 5 -i film.mp4 -t 10 -c copy clip.mp4 # record the screen (stop with q) ffmpeg -f x11grab -framerate 30 -i :0.0 -c:v libx264 -preset ultrafast screen.mp4 %04d means \"four-digit frame number\": render_0001.png , render_0002.png … Change it to match your files. Screenshots and screen recording Print key: screenshot of the whole screen; Alt+Print : the window ( Screen Casting → Screenshot for options). Record the screen with the FFmpeg command above. OBS Studio and SimpleScreenRecorder are not on the ISO in r16; install them with sudo apt install obs-studio or simplescreenrecorder (see Adding software ).", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Sound and music", "s": "Record voices with Audacity, mix the soundtrack in Ardour, compose with LMMS, Hydrogen and MuseScore, and understand how audio works on Animux.", "u": "software/audio.html", "x": "Program Use it to Start Audacity 3.2 record and clean up dialogue and sound effects (easiest) audacity Ardour 7.3 record and mix many tracks, the full soundtrack of a film, with effects and automation ardour LMMS 1.2 compose music with built-in synthesizers, beats and samples lmms Hydrogen 1.2 program drum rhythms hydrogen MuseScore 3 write sheet music and play it back mscore3 Rosegarden MIDI sequencing and scoring rosegarden ZynAddSubFX, Yoshimi, Qsynth, Guitarix, VMPK software instruments, guitar amp, on-screen keyboard menu Sonic Visualiser, Praat look at sound; Praat analyses speech (useful for lip sync timing) menu SoundConverter, SoX convert sound files soundconverter , sox All are in Sound Engineering . Audacity: record a voice-over Audacity. The first time it shows a welcome window with links to help; click OK. Plug in a microphone (a USB microphone or headset is easiest). Choose it in the microphone list on the toolbar. Click the red Record button, speak, click Stop . Clean it: select a quiet part → Effect → Noise Removal and Repair → Noise Reduction → Get Noise Profile ; select everything (Ctrl+A) → Noise Reduction → OK . Then Effect → Volume and Compression → Normalize . File → Export → Export as WAV (best for editing) or MP3. Ardour: mix the soundtrack The first time, Ardour runs a short setup assistant: click Forward through it (the defaults are fine). When Ardour starts it asks for a session (project) and the audio system : choose JACK/PipeWire or ALSA (see below). Add tracks with Track → Add Track, Bus or VCA , import the dialogue, music and effects with Session → Import , and set levels in the Mixer window (Alt+M). Session → Export → Export to Audio File(s) writes the final mix. To line up sound with picture inside Ardour, Session → Open Video needs the extra programs harvid and xjadeo , which are not included. The easier way: edit the final mix against the picture in Kdenlive . LMMS and Hydrogen: make music The first time, LMMS asks to create its working folder in Documents: click Yes. In LMMS , drag an instrument (for example TripleOscillator ) from the left into the Song Editor , double-click its pattern to open the Piano Roll and draw notes. The Beat+Bassline Editor is for drum patterns. File → Export writes WAV or OGG. In Hydrogen , click squares in the pattern grid to place drum hits; chain patterns in the song editor. How audio works on Animux Animux uses PipeWire , which plays the roles of PulseAudio (normal desktop sound) and JACK (professional low-latency audio) at once. Normally you do nothing. Volume and devices : the speaker icon on the panel, and PulseAudio Volume Control ( Sound Engineering → Sound Server Configuration ): choose the output (speakers, HDMI, headphones) and input (microphone) on the Output Devices , Input Devices and Configuration tabs. JACK programs (Ardour, Hydrogen, Qsynth…) connect to PipeWire's JACK service automatically. If a program cannot find JACK, start it with pw-jack , for example pw-jack hydrogen . QjackCtl shows and changes connections between audio programs (the Graph button). Crackling sound while recording Close other programs. In Ardour choose a larger buffer size (512 or 1024 samples). In a virtual machine audio is often unreliable; record on a real computer if you can. Sound for animation: tips Record dialogue before animating, then animate to it (lip sync). Import the WAV in Blender's timeline ( Add → Sound in the Video Sequencer) or in Pencil2D's sound layer. Use 48 kHz WAV files throughout; it is the film and video standard. Free sound effects and music: https://freesound.org/ , https://opengameart.org/ , https://freemusicarchive.org/ . Check each licence.", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Upscayl", "s": "Enlarge images 2× to 4× and make them sharper with AI models that run on your own computer, offline.", "u": "software/upscayl.html", "x": "Upscayl Menu 2D Graphics → Ancillary Tools → Upscayl Version 2.15.0 Command /opt/Upscayl/upscayl Good for Beginner Files PNG, JPEG, WebP Licence AGPL-3.0 Website upscayl.org Upscayl uses AI models (Real-ESRGAN and others) to make images larger and sharper, where normal resizing makes them blurry. Useful for: rendering at half resolution and upscaling, to save render time (check the result closely); old textures and reference photos; concept art and backgrounds. Everything runs on your computer; no image is uploaded. Upscayl: choose an image, a model and the scale, then click Upscayl. Steps Start 2D Graphics → Ancillary Tools → Upscayl (or type /opt/Upscayl/upscayl ). Select Image (or a folder, with Batch Upscayl ). Choose an AI model : Upscayl Standard for photos and renders, Digital Art for drawings and anime. Choose the scale (2×, 3×, 4×) and the output folder. Click Upscayl and wait. Speed Upscayl uses the graphics card through Vulkan . With a real AMD, Intel or NVIDIA graphics card it takes seconds. In a virtual machine or without a Vulkan-capable driver it falls back to the slow software renderer ( lavapipe ), and a large image can take many minutes. For animation frames, upscale the whole image sequence folder with Batch mode, then build the video with FFmpeg ( video tools ). Check a few frames first: AI upscaling can make small details flicker from frame to frame. More AI tools (subtitles, background removal, chat models, Stable Diffusion): AI tools .", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "AI tools", "s": "Speech to text and subtitles (Whisper), background removal (rembg), local chat models (Ollama) and Stable Diffusion (ComfyUI), all running on your own computer.", "u": "software/ai-tools.html", "x": "Animux comes with AI tools that run on your own computer . Nothing you process is uploaded. Some tools are ready to use; the large ones are downloaded only when you want them. Tool What it does Ready? Command Whisper turns speech into text and subtitles yes animux-whisper rembg cuts people and objects out of pictures (removes the background) yes animux-rembg Upscayl makes pictures bigger and sharper yes, in the menu — Ollama chat models (like ChatGPT, but offline) download 1.5 GB sudo animux-install-local-ai --ollama ComfyUI Stable Diffusion: make pictures from text download 0.5–3 GB, plus a model sudo animux-install-local-ai --comfyui LTX in the cloud (Lightricks API) video with sound from text or a picture LTX account + API key; paid per second of video animux-ltx-api LTX-2 on your computer the same, run locally in ComfyUI NVIDIA card with 24–32 GB; about 40 GB of models sudo animux-install-local-ai --ltx-local --cuda Internet and space for the first use Every tool downloads its AI model the first time you use it (from 70 MB to several GB). After that it works offline. In a live session downloads are kept in memory and lost when you switch off; install Animux to keep them. These tools work from the terminal . Open a terminal in the folder with your files (in the file manager: right-click → Open Terminal Here ). Whisper: speech to text and subtitles Whisper listens to an audio or video file and writes down what is said. Use it for dialogue transcripts, lip-sync timing sheets and subtitles. animux-whisper dialogue.wav --model small --language en It writes dialogue.txt , dialogue.srt and other formats into the folder the terminal is in. Video files work too ( scene01.mp4 ). Useful options: Option Meaning --model tiny / base / small / medium / large bigger = more accurate and slower. small is a good start. First download: tiny 72 MB, small 460 MB, large 2.9 GB. --language en the spoken language ( de , fr , es , ja …). Without it Whisper guesses. --output_format srt only the subtitle file. txt = plain text, all = every format. --task translate translate the speech into English. --output_dir subtitles where to put the files. On the Animux test machine (4 processor cores, no graphics card) the tiny model wrote down a 9-second clip in 10 seconds, including the model download. Names it has never heard may be misspelt (\"Animux\" became \"Anomox\"), so check the text. Subtitles in a film: load the .srt file in Kdenlive ( Project → Subtitles → Import Subtitle File ), or burn it in with FFmpeg: ffmpeg -i film.mp4 -vf subtitles=film.srt film-subtitled.mp4 rembg: remove backgrounds rembg cuts out the main subject of a picture and makes the background transparent (a PNG with transparency). Use it for reference photos, collage animation, character turnarounds and compositing. animux-rembg i photo.jpg photo-cutout.png For a whole folder, for example an image sequence: animux-rembg p frames/ frames-cutout/ The first time, rembg downloads its model ( about 1 GB ). On the test machine the first picture took two and a half minutes including the download; later pictures take seconds. Check the edges AI cut-outs can be soft or miss thin parts like hair and fingers. Clean the edge in Krita or GIMP when it matters. In an image sequence the edge may flicker a little from frame to frame. Ollama: chat models on your computer Ollama runs language models offline: ask for story ideas, dialogue variations, names, or help with commands. Install it once (1.5 GB download): sudo animux-install-local-ai --ollama Then start the Ollama service in one terminal and leave that window open: ollama serve In a second terminal, download a model and chat with it (type /bye to stop): ollama run llama3.2 Models are listed at https://ollama.com/library . Small models (1–3 billion parameters, 1–2 GB) answer at reading speed on a normal processor; larger ones need a lot of memory or a graphics card. On the test machine a tiny model ( smollm2:135m ) downloaded and answered within ", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "AI agents in Blender, GIMP, Krita and Inkscape (MCP)", "s": "Let an AI assistant work in Blender, GIMP, Krita and Inkscape through MCP, offline with a local model or with Claude Code; plus Higgsfield and LTX cloud generation.", "u": "software/ai-agents.html", "x": "MCP (Model Context Protocol) lets an AI assistant use programs: it can ask Blender what is in the scene and build or change things, paint and edit images in GIMP and Krita, or draw vector shapes in Inkscape. Animux comes with the pieces in the package animux-mcp : Server What it is Notes blender the official Blender Lab MCP server 1.0.3 (Blender Foundation) scene and file summaries, run code, screenshots, renders, Blender API and manual search blender-community MCP for Blender 2.2.4 by Siddharth Ahuja run code, scene info, viewport look, Poly Haven assets; Animux turns its usage statistics off and its safe mode on gimp GIMP MCP by maorcc (GPL-3.0), for GIMP 3.2 79 tools: new images, layers, selections, colour and filters, text, export; run Python in GIMP; off until animux-mcp gimp on krita Krita MCP by SanSaSane (CC0), for Krita 5.3 documents, layers, drawing, filters, export; run Python in Krita; off until animux-mcp krita on inkscape inkmcp 1.0.0 by Shriinivas (AGPL-3.0), for Inkscape 1.4 create and change SVG elements in the open drawing, document info, PNG preview; run Python (inkex); off until animux-mcp inkscape on animux-guide this guide search and read the guide higgsfield Higgsfield cloud image and video generation needs a Higgsfield account and uses its paid credits; off by default ltx-api LTX video with sound (Lightricks API), animux-ltx-api mcp needs an LTX API key; paid per second; off by default ltx-docs search the LTX API documentation off by default The assistant can run code in your programs All these bridges let the AI run Python inside Blender, GIMP, Krita or Inkscape as you : it can change or delete your work and read and write your files. Save your work first , keep backups, and switch a bridge off when you do not use it ( animux-mcp blender off , animux-mcp gimp off , ...). The bridges only accept connections from your own computer. Blender itself recommends using this in a virtual machine or on a computer without sensitive files. 1. Switch on a Blender add-on Only one of the two Blender add-ons can be on at a time (both use port 9876). In a terminal : animux-mcp blender lab # the official Blender Lab add-on (recommended) animux-mcp blender community # or: MCP for Blender animux-mcp blender off # switch both off again The official add-on needs Allow Online Access in Blender's preferences; animux-mcp switches that on (the add-on only listens on your own computer, 127.0.0.1). Then start Blender . Check that it is ready: animux-mcp status The last line must say Blender MCP bridge: listening on 127.0.0.1:9876 . 1b. Switch on GIMP, Krita or Inkscape Each program gets a small plug-in that runs inside it; animux-mcp installs it for your account only and switches the matching server on in your server list ( ~/.config/animux/mcp/servers.json ). Run these as your normal user, not with sudo. Program Switch on Then GIMP 3.2 animux-mcp gimp on animux-mcp gimp start opens GIMP with the bridge already running (127.0.0.1:9877). Or start GIMP yourself and choose Tools → MCP → Start MCP Server . Krita 5.3 close Krita, then animux-mcp krita on Start Krita: the bridge starts with it (it uses a secret token stored in your Krita folder). Check it with python3 /usr/share/animux/mcp/krita/mcp_server.py --selftest . Inkscape 1.4 animux-mcp inkscape on Start (or restart) Inkscape and open a drawing; the AI works on the drawing that is open. It talks to Inkscape through your desktop session (D-Bus). animux-mcp status shows which bridges are running. Switch one off again with animux-mcp gimp off (or krita off , inkscape off ). These bridges belong to the new GIMP 3.2, Krita 5.3 and Inkscape 1.4 from the Animux repository (packages animux-gimp , animux-krita , animux-inkscape ); they do not work with the older Debian versions. 2a. Offline, with a local model (Ollama) Install Ollama once: sudo animux-install-local-ai --ollama (see AI tools ). Download a model that can use tools , for example ollama pull qwen3:8b (5 GB; needs about 8 G", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Storyboards, print and PDF", "s": "Plan your film with storyboards in Krita, Blender or Scribus, make an animatic, and lay out posters, scripts and portfolios as PDF.", "u": "software/storyboard-publishing.html", "x": "Storyboards A storyboard is your film as a comic: one drawing per shot, with notes on action, camera and sound. Three ways on Animux: Way How Krita File → New → Templates → Comic Templates (panels ready). Or one image per shot in the Storyboard docker ( Settings → Dockers → Storyboard ), which can export a PDF. Blender File → New → Storyboarding : draw with Grease Pencil on frames, with a camera per shot. Play it directly as an animatic. Scribus lay out drawn panels with text boxes for dialogue and notes, export PDF for the team. Animatic An animatic is the storyboard timed to the soundtrack: each drawing is shown as long as its shot will last. It shows whether the story works before you animate. Put the drawings and the recorded dialogue in Kdenlive and adjust the lengths, or use Blender's Storyboarding template. Scribus Publishing → Desktop Publishing → Scribus makes page layouts: storyboard sheets, posters, comics, portfolios, festival press kits. File → New : page size (A4, Letter, or your own), margins. Draw image frames (I) and text frames (T), then File → Import → Get Image / Get Text . File → Export → Save as PDF (choose PDF/X for professional printing). PDF tools Document Viewer (Evince) reads PDFs. PDF Arranger : merge PDFs, reorder, rotate and delete pages by dragging.", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "System tools", "s": "Backups with Timeshift, disks with Disks and GParted, burning with K3b, system monitors, virtual machines, containers and remote access.", "u": "software/system-tools.html", "x": "Backups Tool Backs up Use Timeshift ( Digital Asset Managers ) the system (installed Animux) take a snapshot before big updates; roll back if something breaks a copy to an external disk your projects drag ~/Projects to the disk in the file manager, or use the command below Borg , rsnapshot , rclone (terminal) projects, automatically, also to the cloud for advanced users rsync -a --info=progress2 ~/Projects/ /media/$USER/BACKUP/Projects/ Timeshift does not back up your Home folder by default. That is what the project copy is for. Disks Disks ( System → Disks ): see all disks, check their health ( SMART Data ), format USB sticks, create disk images. GParted ( System → GParted ): resize and create partitions. Danger Both can erase data. Check the disk name and size twice before you click Format or Apply . Burning discs: K3b DVD Authoring → Backup → K3b . New Data Project for a backup DVD, New Video DVD Project if you already have DVD video files, Burn Image to write an ISO to disc. Watching the system Tool Shows Task Manager ( System ) or htop which programs use processor and memory; end a frozen program btop , glances a colourful overview of everything nvidia-smi (NVIDIA driver installed) graphics card use Psensor temperatures. Long renders heat the computer; check that fans work df -h , Disk Usage Analyzer free disk space Virtual machines and containers Animux includes QEMU , Virtual Machine Manager ( System → Virtual Machine Manager ), Docker and Podman for running other systems or packaged services (for example a web server for a render farm dashboard). They are for advanced users. Remote access The SSH server is installed and enabled on installed systems. From another computer: ssh your-login@animux-computer . Useful to start renders on a render node. Change the default live password if a live system is connected to a network you do not trust. Security ufw (firewall), fail2ban , rkhunter and lynis are included. On r16 ( _21 ) the fail2ban service reports an error at start-up (fixed in r16.1, see Troubleshooting ); it does no harm.", "sec": "Software"}, {"t": "Your first 3D render", "s": "In Blender, replace the default cube with a monkey head, give it shiny red paint, add a floor, light it and render a picture. About 30 minutes, no experience needed.", "u": "tutorials/first-3d-render.html", "x": "At the end you will have this picture, made by you: The finished render: Suzanne, Blender's monkey mascot, in red paint on a grey floor. You will learn the basics every 3D program shares: objects, the viewport, materials, lights, the camera and rendering . The finished file If you get stuck, download first-render.blend and open it in Blender ( File → Open ) to compare. 1. Start Blender Animux → 3D Animation → Modellers → Blender . Click outside the welcome screen to close it. You see the default scene: a cube , a camera (the pyramid shape) and a light (the dotted circle). The default scene. Try moving the view (this does not change the scene): Do Mouse Orbit around hold the middle mouse button (the wheel) and move Pan Shift + middle mouse button Zoom turn the wheel No middle button (laptop)? Use the small coloured axes at the top right of the viewport: drag them to orbit, and use the hand and magnifier icons below them. 2. Replace the cube with a monkey Click the cube to select it (it gets an orange outline). Press X and choose Delete (the mouse must be over the 3D view). In the viewport's header click Add → Mesh → Monkey . Suzanne appears where the cube was. Make her smooth: right-click on her → Shade Smooth . Make her rounder: press Ctrl+2 . This adds a Subdivision Surface modifier (you can see it in the Modifiers tab, the blue wrench). Suzanne, smooth and subdivided. 3. Add a floor Add → Mesh → Plane . Make it bigger: press S , type 15 , press Enter . Move it down under Suzanne: press G , then Z , type -1 , press Enter . The keys G (grab), R (rotate) and S (scale), followed by X , Y or Z and a number, are the fastest way to place things. 4. Paint it red Click Suzanne again to select her. In the Properties editor (right side), click the Material tab, the red-and-grey sphere at the bottom of the tab column. Click New . A material called Material with a Principled BSDF is created. Click the white bar next to Base Color and choose a red. Set Roughness to 0.3 for a shiny paint (0 = mirror, 1 = matt). To see colours in the viewport, switch the shading to Material Preview : the third of the four sphere buttons at the top right of the viewport (or press Z → Material Preview ). The Material tab with a red base colour; the viewport in Material Preview. Give the floor a grey-blue material the same way (select the plane, New , pick a colour, Roughness 0.6 ). 5. Light and camera Light : click the light in the Outliner (top right list). In the Object Data tab (the green bulb) set Power to 2500 W . Camera : press Numpad 0 to look through the camera (or View → Cameras → Active Camera ). The scene is already framed. To change the view: View → Navigation → Walk/Fly or, easier, move the view where you like and press Ctrl+Alt+Numpad 0 to move the camera there. 6. Render settings Click the Render tab in Properties (the camera-back icon, second from the top). Render Engine : Cycles . Device : CPU (always works). Under Sampling → Render , set Max Samples to 32 and keep Denoise ticked. More samples = less noise but slower. Render properties: Cycles, CPU, 32 samples. In the Output tab (the printer icon), check Resolution : 1280 × 720 , 100 % . 7. Render! Press F12 (or Render → Render Image ). A new window opens and the picture builds up tile by tile. In a VM or on an older computer it may take a minute. The render window with the finished picture. 8. Save the picture and the scene In the render window : Image → Save As… , choose Pictures , type the name first-render.png , click Save As Image . In the main window : File → Save As… (Ctrl+Shift+S), make a folder Projects , name first-render.blend . Live session In the live session, copy both files to a USB stick before you switch off. See The live session . What next? Change the colour, the light, or add a second object ( Add → Mesh → UV Sphere ), and render again. Make it move: A turntable animation . Learn more: Blender's manual ( Help → Manual ) and the free courses at https://studio.blender.org/tr", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "A turntable animation", "s": "Make Suzanne spin once around in four seconds with two keyframes, then render the animation straight to an MP4 video. About 20 minutes.", "u": "tutorials/turntable-animation.html", "x": "A turntable shows a model from every side: it turns once around. Studios use turntables to present models. You need the scene from Your first 3D render (or download first-render.blend ). This is the result (4 seconds, 24 frames per second): The finished file: turntable.blend . Keyframes, in one sentence You tell Blender where an object is at a few moments ( keyframes ), and Blender calculates every frame in between. 1. Set the length At the bottom, in the Timeline , set Start to 1 and End to 96 . At 24 frames per second that is 4 seconds. 2. First keyframe: frame 1 Make sure the current frame (the blue number in the timeline) is 1 . Select Suzanne . In the Object tab of Properties (orange square), look at Rotation Z . Hover the mouse over the Rotation Z field and press I . The field turns yellow : it has a keyframe on this frame. A diamond appears in the timeline. 3. Second keyframe: one turn later Type 97 in the current-frame box of the timeline (one frame after the end, so the loop has no repeated frame). In Rotation Z , add 360 to the number that is there (for example 40 becomes 400 ) and press Enter . Hover over the field and press I again. The timeline with keyframes; Rotation Z is yellow because it is animated. Press Space to play. Suzanne turns, but she starts slowly, speeds up and slows down again (\"ease in, ease out\"). For a turntable the speed must be even: Move the mouse over the timeline , press A (select all keyframes), then T and choose Linear . Play again with Space : an even, endless spin. Press Space to stop. 4. Render to a video file Open the Output tab (the printer icon). Under Output , set the path to //turntable ( // means \"in the same folder as the .blend file\"). Set the File Format to FFmpeg Video (in Blender 5 first choose the media type Video ). Under Encoding : Container MPEG-4 , Video Codec H.264 , Output Quality Medium quality . The Output tab: resolution, frame range 1–96 and the output path \"//turntable\". File Format and Encoding are further down; scroll the panel. Lower the Max Samples in the Render tab to 16 to make it faster. 96 frames take a while. Render → Render Animation (Ctrl+F12). Blender renders frame after frame. When it is finished, the file turntable0001-0096.mp4 is next to your .blend file. Watch it: double-click it in the file manager (it opens in VLC), or Render → View Animation in Blender. Image sequences are safer For real projects, render to PNG images instead of a video (File Format PNG , path //frames/turntable_ ). If Blender stops at frame 60, you keep frames 1–59 and continue from 60. Join the images into a video with FFmpeg or Kdenlive afterwards ( how ). Render without opening Blender Close Blender and render from a terminal; it is faster and you can keep working: blender -b ~/Projects/turntable.blend -a See Command-line rendering . Next Animate other properties the same way: location (a jumping ball), scale, colour (hover over any field and press I ). A character with MakeHuman .", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "A character with MakeHuman", "s": "Create a human character with MPFB inside Blender, give it a skeleton, pose it and set a keyframe, or design it in MakeHuman II and bring it over. About 30 minutes.", "u": "tutorials/character-makehuman.html", "x": "Modelling a believable human by hand takes weeks. With MPFB (in Blender) or MakeHuman II (on its own) you get one in minutes, with a skeleton for animation. See MakeHuman II and MPFB for the background. Way 1: MPFB, directly in Blender (recommended) 1. Create the human Start Blender . Delete the cube ( X → Delete ). Move the mouse over the 3D viewport and press N : the side panel opens on the right. Click its MPFB tab. Open the New human section, then From scratch , and click Create human . A new MPFB human in Blender. The side panel (N) is open; its vertical tabs at the right edge are Item, Tool, View, Animation and **MPFB v2.0.18**: click MPFB. Press Numpad . (period) to zoom to the selected human. 2. Shape the body Open Model in the MPFB tab. The Macrodetails sliders change the whole body: gender , age , muscle , weight , height , proportions . The other groups (head, face, torso, arms, legs) change details. Move a slider and watch the body change. 3. Skin, eyes and hair Open Apply assets and pick a skin , eyes , eyebrows and eyelashes from its lists. Hair and clothes come from asset packs (see the note on asset packs ). Switch the viewport to Material Preview (Z → Material Preview) to see the skin. 4. Add a skeleton With the human selected, open Rigging → Standard rig and click Add standard rig . An armature (skeleton) appears inside the body, and the body follows it. For more animation controls use Rigify instead ( Rigging → Rigify ), then Generate rigify rig . 5. Pose and animate Click the armature (in the Outliner it is called Human.rig or similar). Switch to Pose Mode : Ctrl+Tab (or the mode menu at the top left of the viewport). Click a bone, for example the upper arm, and rotate it: R , move the mouse, click. Set a keyframe: press I with the mouse in the viewport. Go to frame 24, pose the arm differently, press I again. Press Space to play. MPFB can also load ready poses and animations ( Rigging → Poses / Animation ), including Mixamo-compatible animation ( Rigging → Snap to Mixamo ). Way 2: design in MakeHuman II, animate in Blender Start 3D Animation → Modellers → MakeHuman 2 , keep the base mesh fantasy , and shape the character with the modelling sliders. Save it: File → Save as my-hero.mhm . In Blender's MPFB tab: New human → From save file , choose my-hero.mhm . Or export from MakeHuman II as GLB and import it in Blender with File → Import → glTF 2.0 . That brings the mesh and skeleton, but MPFB's sliders are then no longer available. MakeHuman II. Tips Keep characters at real size (a person is about 1.7 m in Blender). Save often: Ctrl+S . For lip sync, MPFB's Face operations offer Load face shape keys and Assign Lip Sync shape keys .", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "A bouncing ball in Synfig", "s": "The classic first animation exercise. Make a ball fall, squash and bounce with waypoints and ease in and out, then export a GIF. About 30 minutes.", "u": "tutorials/bouncing-ball-synfig.html", "x": "The bouncing ball teaches three of the twelve principles of animation : timing , slow in and slow out , and squash and stretch . Animux has a finished version as a sample: /usr/share/animux/samples/synfig/bouncing-ball.sif . The sample bouncing ball open in Synfig Studio. 1. A new document Start 2D Animation → Synfig Studio . Canvas → Properties (or the Canvas MetaData button): on the Time tab set End Time to 24 (one second at 24 fps). Click OK . 2. Draw the ground and the ball Pick the Rectangle tool and drag a long, thin rectangle at the bottom: the ground. Pick the Circle tool , choose a colour in the toolbox's Fill colour box, and drag a circle near the top: the ball. 3. Animate the fall and bounce Click the Animation mode button at the bottom right of the canvas. The canvas gets a red border : from now on every change is recorded. Frame 0 : the ball is at the top. Select the circle layer, and in Params right-click Origin → Add Waypoint (or simply move the ball a tiny bit). Move the time slider to frame 12 . Drag the ball down until it touches the ground. A waypoint appears in the Timetrack . Move to frame 24 and drag the ball back to the top. Press Play (or drag the time slider). The ball goes down and up. 4. Make it feel heavy: slow in and out A real ball is slow at the top and fast at the bottom . In the Timetrack, right-click the waypoints at frames 0 and 24 → Both → Ease In/Out (slow), and the one at frame 12 → Both → Linear (fast, sharp bounce). Play again: it looks much more natural. 5. Squash and stretch At the moment of impact the ball squashes. Add this with a Scale or Stretch layer above the circle ( Layer → New Layer → Distortions → Stretch ): frame 10: amount 1.0, 1.1 (a little stretched while falling fast); frame 12: amount 1.3, 0.7 (squashed on the ground); frame 14: back to 1.0, 1.0 . Set the stretch's centre at the ball's bottom. 6. Export File → Render : file name ball.gif , Target gif , click Render . Or for editing in Kdenlive: target png (one image per frame). From a terminal: synfig ~/ball.sifz -t gif -o ~/ball.gif Turn Animation mode off before you continue drawing, and save ( File → Save , .sifz ). Next Make the ball bounce several times, lower each time; add a shadow that grows as the ball comes down. Then try a character with bones ( Skeleton layer ), see https://synfig.readthedocs.io/ .", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "Hand-drawn animation in Pencil2D", "s": "Draw a short flip-book animation frame by frame with onion skin, play it back and export it as GIF or MP4. About 30 minutes, perfect for beginners and children.", "u": "tutorials/hand-drawn-pencil2d.html", "x": "Traditional animation is a series of drawings shown quickly. Pencil2D makes it as easy as a flip book. Pencil2D: tools on the left, colours on the right, the timeline with layers at the bottom. 1. Get ready Start 2D Animation → Pencil2D . A new project with a Camera , a Vector and a Bitmap layer opens. Click the Bitmap layer in the timeline (that is where you draw like on paper). Pick the Pencil tool (N) and a dark colour. With a drawing tablet, pressure works automatically. 2. Key drawings Animators start with the keys , the most important poses. We will animate a ball jumping across the screen. Frame 1 : draw the ball on the left, on the ground. Click frame 7 in the Bitmap layer, then the + button ( Add Frame ) above the layers. Draw the ball high in the middle. Frame 13 : add a frame and draw the ball on the right, on the ground. 3. In-betweens with onion skin Turn on onion skin : the onion buttons above the timeline ( previous and next ). The drawings before and after appear faintly in red and blue. Click frame 4 , add a frame, and draw the ball halfway between frame 1 and 7: a little lower than half the height (the ball is faster near the ground). Do the same on frame 10 . Each drawing is held until the next one: frame 1 shows until frame 3, and so on. That is animating on threes . 4. Play it Press Play (or Enter ). Change the speed in the FPS box next to the play buttons: 12 is classic for hand-drawn animation. Switch on Loop to watch it repeat. Too jerky? Add more in-betweens (frames 2, 3, 5, 6…) and animate on twos or ones. 5. Colour Pencil2D's Bucket (K) fills closed areas. For clean colouring, draw lines on a Vector layer: its bucket fill follows the lines exactly, even after you edit them. 6. Export File → Export → Movie : choose MP4 (for Kdenlive or sharing) or GIF (for the web) and the size. File → Export → Image Sequence : PNG files, the best quality for editing. Save your project with File → Save ( .pclx ). Next steps Add a Sound layer ( Layer → New Sound Layer ) to animate to music or a voice. Use the Camera layer to pan across a wide background. The twelve principles of animation (Frank Thomas and Ollie Johnston, The Illusion of Life ) are the best next thing to learn. The bouncing ball in Synfig teaches the first three.", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "Paint an animation in Krita", "s": "Use Krita's animation workspace to paint a short loop, such as a flickering candle flame, with onion skins, then render it to a video. About 40 minutes.", "u": "tutorials/krita-animation.html", "x": "Krita animates like Pencil2D but with all its painting brushes, so it is good for painterly effects : fire, water, smoke, magic. We paint a candle flame that loops. Krita's welcome screen. 1. A new animation document Start 2D Graphics → Bitmap Editors → Krita . File → New : width 1080 , height 1080 , Create . Window → Workspace → Animation . The Timeline (bottom), Animation and Onion Skins panels appear. In the Animation panel set Frame rate 12 , Start 0 , End 11 (one-second loop). 2. Background and candle Fill the background layer dark (bucket fill, F ). Add a layer ( Insert ) named Candle and paint the candle body. It does not move, so it needs no frames. 3. The flame, frame by frame Add a new layer Flame . In the timeline, right-click frame 0 of Flame → Create Blank Frame . Paint a flame with a soft brush (orange-yellow, brighter in the middle). Turn on the onion skin (the light bulb icon on the Flame layer in the timeline). Click frame 2 , right-click → Create Blank Frame , and paint the flame a little taller and leaning left. Continue on frames 4, 6, 8 and 10 with small changes. Each drawing holds for two frames (animating on twos). Make frame 10 lead back to frame 0, so the loop is smooth. 4. Play and adjust Press Play in the Animation panel, and switch on looping . Drag frames in the timeline to change the timing. 5. Glow Duplicate the Flame layer, put the copy below it, apply Filter → Blur → Gaussian Blur (large radius) to each of its frames and set its blending mode to Add or Screen . The flame now glows. 6. Render File → Render Animation : Image Sequence (PNG): best for editing in Kdenlive; Video : choose MP4 and the output file. Krita uses FFmpeg (included). The first time, Krita may ask for the FFmpeg location: /usr/bin/ffmpeg . Save the project as .kra .", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "Stop motion with a webcam", "s": "Set up a tiny studio, capture a clay or LEGO animation with Stopmotion's onion skin, and turn it into a video with sound.", "u": "tutorials/stop-motion-webcam.html", "x": "Your studio in 10 minutes Need Tip A webcam any USB webcam; test it first in VLC ( Media → Open Capture Device ) Something to hold it still a tripod, a clamp, or tape it to a pile of books. It must not move at all. Light close the curtains, use two desk lamps. Daylight changes and makes the film flicker. A stage a table against a wall; a sheet of paper as background Characters plasticine, LEGO, paper cut-outs, toys Capture Start StopMotion Animation → Stopmotion . Settings → Preferences → Video Import : select your camera ( /dev/video0 for the first webcam). Click the camera button: the live picture appears. Set onion skin to show the last 1–2 frames (the slider in the camera bar). Place your character. Take the first frame. Move the character a very small step (a few millimetres), check against the onion skin, take the next frame. Repeat. 12 frames = 1 second of film at 12 fps. Stopmotion. Planning A 10-second scene at 12 fps needs 120 photos. Plan the movement first; count the steps. Moving characters slowly at the start and end of a motion (small steps) and faster in the middle (bigger steps) makes it look natural. Check and fix Press Play . Delete bad frames (a hand in the picture!) by selecting them and pressing Delete . Use the frame rate setting to change the speed. Export File → Export → Video , or export the frames as images and make a video with FFmpeg: ffmpeg -framerate 12 -i frame_%04d.jpg -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -crf 18 my-film.mp4 Add music and titles in Kdenlive . No webcam? Use a phone camera on a small stand. Take the photos, copy them to Animux (USB cable or a card reader), and import them in Stopmotion with Edit → Add frames (or drag them in), or put them straight into Kdenlive as an image sequence.", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "Edit your film in Kdenlive", "s": "Put rendered shots, a title and music together on a timeline and export an MP4 ready for YouTube. Uses the turntable video from the earlier tutorial. About 30 minutes.", "u": "tutorials/edit-video-kdenlive.html", "x": "You need some clips: the turntable0001-0096.mp4 from A turntable animation (or download turntable.mp4 ), and any music file (WAV, MP3 or OGG). Kdenlive with the turntable video in the project bin. 1. Project settings Start NLV Editors → Kdenlive . Project → Project Settings : choose a profile matching your animation, here HD 720p 24 fps (1280 × 720, 24 frames per second). Click OK . Always choose the profile first If the profile does not match your clips, Kdenlive offers to change it when you add the first clip. Say yes if it suggests the clip's own format. 2. Bring in the clips Drag files from the file manager into the Project Bin , or right-click in the bin → Add Clip or Folder . For an image sequence (frames from Blender, Synfig or Krita), choose the first image and tick Import image sequence . 3. Build the edit Drag the turntable clip from the bin onto the V1 track of the timeline. Drag it again so it plays twice in a row (two turns). Click on the timeline to move the playhead; press Space to play in the Project Monitor . To cut: put the playhead where you want the cut and press Shift+R . Delete a part by selecting it and pressing Delete . 4. A title Right-click the Project Bin → Add Title Clip . Type the title (for example Suzanne Spins ), choose font and size, click OK . Drag the title clip onto V2 above the start of the video. It is shown on top. Fade it: right-click the title on the timeline → Add Effect → Fade → Fade In and Fade Out . 5. Music Drag your music into the bin, then onto the A1 audio track. Trim it to the length of the video by dragging its end. Fade out at the end: Add Effect → Fade → Fade Out . Too loud? Add the Volume (keyframable) effect and lower it. 6. Export Project → Render (Ctrl+Enter). Choose the preset MP4-H264/AAC . Set the Output file , for example ~/Videos/suzanne-spins.mp4 . Click Render to File . The Job Queue tab shows progress. Watch the result in VLC. Upload it, send it, or show it. Save the project File → Save writes a .kdenlive file. It only refers to your clips; keep the clips where they are (in your project folder) or Kdenlive cannot find them later.", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "Command-line rendering and batch jobs", "s": "Render Blender, Synfig and POV-Ray without opening them, keep renders running after you log out, render overnight, join frames into a video and convert hundreds of images at once.", "u": "tutorials/command-line-rendering.html", "x": "Rendering from the terminal is faster (no window to draw), uses less memory, and can run while you work or sleep. The same commands are what render farms and AI agents use. Blender blender -b shot.blend -a # the whole animation, as set in the file blender -b shot.blend -s 1 -e 48 -a # frames 1 to 48 blender -b shot.blend -f 1 # only frame 1 blender -b shot.blend -o //frames/sh010_#### -F PNG -a # choose output path and format blender -b shot.blend -E CYCLES -a -- --cycles-device CPU blender -b shot.blend --python-expr \"import bpy; bpy.context.scene.cycles.samples=16\" -a -b (background) must come first , the .blend file next, then the options in order; -a or -f at the end (Blender acts on the options in order). #### becomes the frame number with four digits: sh010_0001.png . // means \"relative to the .blend file\". Keep it running after you close the terminal nohup blender -b shot.blend -a > render.log 2>&1 & tail -f render.log # watch progress (Ctrl+C stops watching, not the render) Or use tmux : type tmux , start the render, press Ctrl+B then D to leave it running; tmux attach brings it back. Render overnight, then switch off blender -b shot.blend -a && systemctl poweroff (Make sure the computer will not go to sleep first: Settings → Power .) Several shots in a row for f in ~/Projects/my-short/04-shots/sh*.blend; do blender -b \"$f\" -a done Use all processor cores, or leave some free Blender uses every core. To keep the computer usable while rendering, lower its priority: nice -n 19 blender -b shot.blend -a . To limit threads: blender -b shot.blend -t 4 -a . Other programs synfig ball.sifz -t png -o frames/ball.png # Synfig: all frames povray +Iscene.pov +Oframes/f.png +W1920 +H1080 +A -D +KFI1 +KFF96 # POV-Ray: frames 1–96 (clock) inkscape title.svg --export-type=png --export-width=1920 # Inkscape openscad -o part.stl part.scad # OpenSCAD gaffer execute -script lighting.gfr -nodes Render -frames 1-24 # Gaffer Frames into a video ffmpeg -framerate 24 -i frames/sh010_%04d.png -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -crf 18 sh010.mp4 Convert and check many images mogrify -path small -resize 50% -format jpg frames/*.png # half-size JPEG copies in ./small oiiotool frames/sh010_0001.exr -o check.png # one EXR → PNG ls frames | wc -l # how many frames are there? Check that everything still works animux-selftest blender povray synfig See the self-test . The command reference for agents lists the command-line use of every program.", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "From idea to finished short film", "s": "The stages of an animated film production and which Animux program to use at each one, with a realistic plan for a first 30-second film.", "u": "tutorials/short-film-workflow.html", "x": "Every animated film, from a school project to a feature, goes through the same stages. Knowing them helps you choose the right program and avoid redoing work. Idea Script Storyboard Animatic Design Assets Animation Lighting & render Compositing Edit & sound Export The stages and the tools Stage What you make 2D film 3D film Stop motion 1. Idea and script a one-sentence idea, then a short script Mousepad, Gedit 2. Storyboard a drawing per shot Krita , paper + scanner, Blender's Storyboarding template same same 3. Animatic storyboard timed with dialogue Kdenlive Blender Kdenlive 4. Voice recording dialogue as WAV Audacity Audacity Audacity 5. Design characters, colours, backgrounds Krita, Inkscape Krita, MakeHuman clay, paper 6. Assets finished characters and sets Synfig cut-outs, Krita backgrounds Blender models, rigs, materials puppets, sets 7. Animation the movement, shot by shot Pencil2D , Synfig , Krita Blender Stopmotion 8. Lighting and rendering final frames as images export frames Blender Cycles/EEVEE, render farm your photos 9. Compositing effects, colour correction Krita, Blender compositor Blender compositor, Gaffer Blender compositor 10. Edit and sound the film with music, effects, titles Kdenlive + Ardour same same 11. Export MP4 for the web, high-quality master Kdenlive, HandBrake same same Your first 30-second film: a realistic plan Week Do 1 Idea (one character, one place, one problem). Script of half a page. Storyboard with 8–12 shots. 2 Record the voices or choose music. Make the animatic in Kdenlive and watch it ten times. Change what does not work now . 3 Design and build: character, background(s). 4–6 Animate, shot by shot. Start with the hardest shot. Show it to someone every few days. 7 Render (overnight), colour, final edit, sound mix, titles and credits. Export. Keep it small Thirty seconds is 720 frames at 24 fps. One character and one place is plenty. Finished and short beats long and unfinished. Rules that save days One folder per project , with the layout studios use . Name shots by number : sh010 , sh020 … (leaving gaps lets you add sh015 later). Render to image sequences (PNG or EXR), not to video. Edit in Kdenlive. Lock the frame rate (24 or 25 fps) at the start and use it everywhere. Back up to an external drive every day. Test render small (25–50 % resolution, few samples) before the final render. Credits and licences If you use music, sounds, fonts or models made by others, check that their licence allows it, and credit them. CC0 means free for everything; CC-BY means free with credit.", "sec": "Tutorials"}, {"t": "The self-test and sample scenes", "s": "Check in a few minutes that every renderer and the main programs work on your computer, read the results, and use the sample scenes to learn.", "u": "help/self-test.html", "x": "Animux Tools → Animux Self-Test renders a small scene with every renderer, opens the most important file formats and checks that the main programs start. It takes 2–10 minutes. The self-test result in VirtualBox: 29 passed, 0 failed. Reading the results Result Means Do PASS the program ran and produced a correct result nothing FAIL the program is installed but did not work open the log file named in the line (see below) SKIP not installed, or this processor is too old for it \"this processor has no SSE4.2/AVX\": see Requirements All pictures, files and logs are kept in ~/animux-selftest/<date-time>/ ; the menu entry opens that folder at the end. summary.txt there has the result list. Attach that folder when you ask for help . The tests Test name Program What it checks blender Blender Cycles renders a small scene apps MakeHuman II the program and its Qt libraries load povray POV-Ray renders samples/povray/animux-spheres.pov appleseed appleseed renders the bundled helloworld project luxcore LuxCoreRender renders samples/luxcore/render.py mitsuba Mitsuba 3 renders samples/mitsuba/render.py pbrt pbrt-v4 renders samples/pbrt/animux-spheres.pbrt gaffer Gaffer Cycles render from a Gaffer node graph usd OpenUSD tools (usdview, usdcat …) reads samples/usd/animux-scene.usda, writes .usdc alembic Alembic tools (abcls, abctree …) lists samples/alembic/animux-cube.abc exr OpenEXR and OpenImageIO tools converts PNG to EXR and reads the header ocio OpenColorIO tools validates samples/ocio/config.ocio vdb OpenVDB tools makes a level-set sphere and reads it back flamenco Flamenco manager and worker start farmerjoe Farmerjoe the Perl script compiles opentoonz OpenToonz renders samples/opentoonz/animux-lines with tcomposer synfig Synfig Studio renders frame 12 of samples/synfig/bouncing-ball.sif apps DJV the program starts The apps test also checks that MakeHuman 2, DJV, Avidemux and Cinelerra-GG start. From the terminal animux-selftest # everything animux-selftest --list # list the test names animux-selftest blender povray # only these animux-selftest --open # open the results folder at the end The exit code is 0 when nothing failed, 1 otherwise, so scripts and AI agents can use it as a health check. Sample scenes Animux Tools → Animux Sample Scenes opens /usr/share/animux/samples : Folder File Open or run with povray/ animux-spheres.pov menu Renderers → POV-Ray , or povray +Ianimux-spheres.pov +O/tmp/out.png +W800 +H600 blender/ turntable.py blender -b -P turntable.py -- /tmp/out.png synfig/ bouncing-ball.sif Synfig Studio ( File → Open ), or synfig bouncing-ball.sif -t gif -o /tmp/ball.gif mitsuba/ render.py animux-vfx-python render.py /tmp/out.exr luxcore/ render.py animux-vfx-python render.py /tmp/out.png gaffer/ render.py gaffer env python render.py /tmp/out.exr pbrt/ animux-spheres.pbrt pbrt --outfile /tmp/out.exr animux-spheres.pbrt usd/ animux-scene.usda usdview animux-scene.usda alembic/ animux-cube.abc Blender: File → Import → Alembic ; abcls -r animux-cube.abc ocio/ config.ocio ociocheck --iconfig config.ocio The sample files are read-only. Copy one to your Home folder before changing it: cp -r /usr/share/animux/samples ~/samples . More examples: /usr/share/povray-3.7/scenes (hundreds of POV-Ray scenes), /opt/animux/gaffer/resources (Gaffer), /opt/animux/appleseed/examples (appleseed), and the tutorial files on this guide: first-render.blend , turntable.blend .", "sec": "Help"}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Solutions for black screens, slow virtual machines, programs that do not start, no sound, no Wi-Fi, full memory and lost files.", "u": "help/troubleshooting.html", "x": "Start with the self-test : it often tells you exactly what is wrong. Black screen after the boot menu Wait two minutes : a slow USB stick or a VM can take that long the first time. Restart and choose Workstation (KMS / no nomodeset) in the boot menu . This uses the graphics card's own driver. In VirtualBox: Settings → Display → Graphics Controller: VMSVGA , Video Memory 128 MB, 3D acceleration off . Choose Workstation (verbose / no Plymouth) to see the messages, or DEBUG: Text Console to log in as user / user and look at the logs: journalctl -b -p err . The screen resolution is wrong or too small Settings → Display . If the resolution you want is missing, start with Workstation (KMS / no nomodeset) . In VirtualBox the window does not resize automatically (no Guest Additions); see VirtualBox . Blender or a renderer does not start \"Illegal instruction\" , or Blender closes at once: the processor (or the virtual machine) does not offer SSE4.2 or AVX . Check with grep -o -w -E 'sse4_2|avx' /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u . In QEMU add -cpu host ; in other VMs choose \"host CPU\" passthrough. On a real computer from before about 2011 these programs cannot run. Blender starts but the viewport is black or very slow: in a VM this is normal for EEVEE (software OpenGL). Use Workbench or Cycles (CPU). Start it from a terminal to see the error message: type blender and read the last lines. Everything is slow Where Fix In a VM give it more memory (8 GB+) and processors (4+). Close programs on the host. Live session from USB use a USB 3 stick in a USB 3 port (blue), or install Animux. During rendering normal: rendering uses every core. Lower the priority: nice -n 19 blender -b … Blender viewport use Solid shading while animating; lower Viewport subdivision levels. Watch what uses the computer: System → Task Manager or htop . Memory is full / programs crash in the live session In the live session, files you save are kept in memory. Delete old renders, save to a USB drive instead, or install Animux. Check: free -h and df -h / . No sound Click the speaker icon: is it muted? Turn the volume up. Sound Engineering → Sound Server Configuration → PulseAudio Volume Control → Output Devices and Configuration : choose the right device (speakers, HDMI, headphones) and profile. A JACK program (Ardour, Hydrogen) is silent: start it with pw-jack ardour . No Wi-Fi Click the network icon at the top right. Is Wi-Fi enabled? Is the network listed? Some Wi-Fi chips need firmware Animux does not have, or are switched off with a key (airplane mode) on the laptop. Show the chip: lspci -nnk | grep -A3 -i network and search the web for its name plus \"Debian 12\". A USB Wi-Fi stick or a cable (or phone USB tethering) always works. A USB stick or disk does not appear Unplug and plug it in again; look in the file manager's left panel. A Windows disk from a computer with Fast Startup may be locked: shut Windows down fully (hold Shift while clicking Shut down ). I forgot my password (installed system) Restart; in the start menu choose Advanced options → recovery mode , then root and type passwd your-login-name . Then reboot . \"No bootable option\" after installing (UEFI) An Animux installed on a UEFI computer or VM up to r16 may not start when the disk is moved to another computer or VM, or when the firmware forgets its boot list: the screen says No bootable option or device was found . The installer wrote Animux's start file only to EFI/Animux/ , not to the standard fallback place EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI . To start it once: open the firmware menu (press a key at that message, or F2 / Del at start-up), choose Boot Maintenance Manager → Boot From File (the name differs between makers), then the disk → EFI → Animux → grubx64.efi . To fix it for good, in Animux open a terminal and type: sudo grub-install --target=x86_64-efi --efi-directory=/boot/efi --removable From then on the disk starts on any UEFI computer. (Tested in VirtualBox with an installed r16. Animux r16.1 ( _25 ) and later do", "sec": "Help"}, {"t": "Questions and answers", "s": "Short answers to the questions new Animux users ask most often.", "u": "help/faq.html", "x": "Is Animux free? Can I use it for paid work? Yes and yes. Animux and its programs are free and open source. Your films, images and models belong to you. See Licences . Do I have to know Linux? No. This guide explains everything from the start. You need the terminal for only a few jobs, and the guide gives the full command each time. Will Animux delete Windows? Not unless you tell the installer to. The live session and virtual machines change nothing. When installing , choose Install alongside to keep Windows. Can I open my Maya / 3ds Max / Cinema 4D / After Effects files? Not directly. Export them from the old program as FBX , OBJ , USD or Alembic and import them in Blender. Images and videos (PNG, EXR, MP4) work everywhere. Does Animux have Photoshop, Toon Boom, Maya or After Effects? No, those are commercial programs for Windows and macOS. Animux has free alternatives: Krita/GIMP (painting, photos), Pencil2D/Synfig/Krita (2D animation), Blender (3D, compositing), Kdenlive (editing). DaVinci Resolve can be installed: Adding software . Which program should I learn first? For 2D: Pencil2D , then Krita or Synfig. For 3D: Blender . For editing: Kdenlive. Follow the tutorials in order. Can Blender render on my graphics card? With an NVIDIA card and NVIDIA's driver (CUDA/OptiX) or an AMD card with ROCm (HIP), yes. Without them, Blender renders on the processor, which always works. See Adding software . Why is Blender 5 and not Debian's Blender 3.4? Debian 12's Blender is from 2022. Animux packages the official Blender 5.2 LTS release from blender.org. Can I use a drawing tablet? Yes. Wacom, Huion, XP-Pen and others work with pressure in Krita, GIMP, MyPaint, Pencil2D and Blender. Do my files survive a restart? In the live session: no . Installed: yes. See The live session . How do I update Animux? sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade on an installed system. See Updates . Can several computers render together? Yes, with Flamenco . Does Animux run on a Mac? On Intel Macs in VirtualBox, VMware Fusion or Parallels, and from USB on many models. On Apple Silicon (M1–M4) only through very slow emulation. Is Animux safe? Does it send my data anywhere? Animux has no tracking. Programs connect to the internet only for things you ask for (updates, web browsing, downloading Blender extensions). Upscayl's AI runs offline. Where is OpenToonz / Natron / OBS Studio? OpenToonz is on Animux from r16.3 ( 2D Animation → OpenToonz ); on older releases get it from Flathub. Natron and OBS Studio are not on the ISO. OBS Studio: sudo apt install obs-studio . Natron: from Flathub, see Adding software . What do the numbers in the ISO name mean? animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso is build number 28 of Genesis64; it is release r16.3 (build 27 was r16.2, build 25 was r16.1, build 21 was r16).", "sec": "Help"}, {"t": "Glossary", "s": "Plain-language explanations of the computer and animation words used in this guide.", "u": "help/glossary.html", "x": "Computer words apt – the command that installs and updates software on Debian and Animux. BIOS / UEFI – the small program inside a computer that starts before the operating system. UEFI is the modern kind; Secure Boot is a UEFI feature that may need to be switched off for Animux. Boot – starting a computer. Boot menu : the list where you choose what to start. CPU (processor) – the computer's main chip. Rendering speed depends a lot on its number of cores . Debian – the Linux distribution Animux is built on. Distribution (distro) – a complete Linux system with programs, like Animux. Firmware – small programs needed by hardware such as Wi-Fi chips and graphics cards. GPU (graphics card) – the chip that draws the screen; also used for fast 3D and rendering. Home folder ( ~ ) – your personal folder, /home/your-name . ISO file – a whole disc in one file. Linux – the free operating system kernel at the heart of Animux, Android and most servers. Live session – Animux running from the USB stick or ISO without installing; nothing is kept after a restart. Package – a program prepared for installing with apt (a .deb file). Partition – a section of a disk. A disk can hold several systems in separate partitions. RAM (memory) – fast working memory; lost when the computer is switched off. Repository – an online store of packages that apt installs from. sudo – run one command as the administrator (root). Terminal – a window for typing commands. Virtual machine (VM) – a pretend computer that runs inside your real one. Xfce – the desktop environment of Animux (panel, menu, windows). Animation and 3D words Animatic – a storyboard timed with sound, to check the story before animating. Armature / rig – the skeleton inside a character that animators move. Bitmap / raster – a picture made of pixels (PNG, JPEG). Gets blurry when enlarged. Codec – the method used to compress a video (H.264, ProRes) or sound (AAC). Compositing – combining rendered layers, effects and colour correction into the final picture. EXR – a high-quality image format with many layers and true light values, used in film. Frame – one picture of a film. fps – frames per second (24 for film, 25 in Europe for TV, 30 for web/US). Image sequence – one image file per frame ( shot_0001.png , shot_0002.png …), the safest way to render. In-between (tween) – the drawings between two key poses. Tweening = the computer makes them. Keyframe – a frame where you set a value (position, rotation…); the program interpolates between keyframes. Material / shader – how a surface looks: colour, shininess, transparency. Mesh – a 3D object made of points (vertices), edges and faces (polygons). Modifier – a non-destructive operation on an object in Blender (smoothing, mirror, array…). Onion skin – seeing the previous and next frames faintly while drawing. Render – calculating the final images from a scene. Render farm : many computers rendering together. Samples – how many light paths a renderer traces per pixel. More samples = less noise, slower. Storyboard – the film drawn as a sequence of panels, one per shot. Timeline – the strip of frames along which you animate or edit. Turntable – an animation of a model turning once around. USD, Alembic, FBX, glTF, OBJ – 3D file formats for moving scenes and models between programs. Vector – a picture made of shapes and curves (SVG); stays sharp at any size. Waypoint – Synfig's name for a keyframe on one parameter.", "sec": "Help"}, {"t": "Getting help", "s": "Where to ask questions about Animux and its programs, and what information to include so others can help you quickly.", "u": "help/getting-help.html", "x": "For questions about a program Each program has a large community, usually the fastest help: Program Help Blender https://blender.stackexchange.com/ , https://blenderartists.org/ , the manual under Help → Manual Krita https://krita-artists.org/ Synfig https://forums.synfig.org/ Pencil2D https://discuss.pencil2d.org/ Kdenlive https://discuss.kde.org/tag/kdenlive MakeHuman / MPFB http://www.makehumancommunity.org/forum/ Flamenco https://flamenco.blender.org/ (chat and docs) Gaffer https://groups.google.com/g/gaffer-dev Debian (the base system) https://wiki.debian.org/ , https://forums.debian.net/ Questions about Debian 12 (\"bookworm\") apply to Animux too, as it is based on it. For problems with Animux itself Collect this information first: The Animux release : run cat /etc/os-release | grep PRETTY and name the ISO file (for example animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso ). Live, VM or installed? Which VM program? Your computer: inxi -Fxz prints the hardware without personal data. The self-test folder: run Animux Tools → Animux Self-Test and attach ~/animux-selftest/<date>/summary.txt and the .log of the failing test. For start-up problems: journalctl -b -p err > ~/errors.txt and attach errors.txt . What you did, what you expected, what happened instead. A screenshot helps ( Print key). Learning Blender Studio training: https://studio.blender.org/training/ Krita manual and tutorials: https://docs.krita.org/ The Illusion of Life (Thomas and Johnston) and The Animator's Survival Kit (Richard Williams), the classic books on animation. This guide's tutorials .", "sec": "Help"}, {"t": "The guide for AI agents and programs", "s": "Where an agent finds this guide on an Animux system (files, a local web server, llms.txt, JSON indexes, a command-line tool and an MCP server) and a recommended way to use it.", "u": "agents/overview.html", "x": "Every Animux system carries this guide in a form that both people and software can use. An AI agent that operates Animux (opening programs, rendering, converting files, answering a user's questions) should use it as its reference for what is installed and how to drive it . Nothing here needs the internet. Where it is Form Location Best for Folder /usr/share/animux/docs/ direct file reading Local web server http://127.0.0.1:8765/ (service animux-docs.service , local only) HTTP clients, browsers llms.txt /usr/share/animux/docs/llms.txt · http://127.0.0.1:8765/llms.txt the entry point for language models (format of https://llmstxt.org/ ) All pages in one file llms-full.txt loading the whole guide into a context window (about 60 pages) Each page as Markdown md/<section>/<page>.md , for example md/tutorials/first-3d-render.md reading one page cleanly Page index api/pages.json id, title, summary, section, tags, paths of every page Software catalogue api/software.json every program: id, version, package, menu path, command, headless commands , file formats, self-test name Command-line tool animux-docs search and read from a shell MCP server animux-docs mcp (stdio) agents that speak the Model Context Protocol The same pages for people, in Firefox on Animux. The HTML pages link to their Markdown twin with <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/markdown\"> . The animux-docs command animux-docs # open the guide in the browser animux-docs open tutorials/first-3d-render animux-docs search render farm # ranked list: id, title, summary animux-docs show agents/system-facts # print a page as Markdown animux-docs software # list the catalogue animux-docs software blender --json # one program as JSON animux-docs path # print the docs folder animux-docs serve --port 8765 # serve it yourself (127.0.0.1 only) animux-docs mcp # MCP server on stdin/stdout search , show and software accept --json for machine-readable output. The MCP server animux-docs mcp is a Model Context Protocol server (JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio, protocol version 2025-06-18). It needs nothing but Python 3 and reads the files above. Tools: Tool Arguments Returns search_docs query (text), limit (optional, default 8) matching pages with id, title, summary and a snippet read_doc id (for example software/blender ) the page as Markdown list_docs section (optional) page ids, titles and summaries list_software category (optional) programs with id, name, version, command get_software id (for example blender ) the full catalogue entry, with headless commands system_info — Animux release, live or installed, processor features, display, docs location Pages are also offered as MCP resources ( animux-docs://<id> , Markdown). Example configuration for an MCP client (Claude Desktop, Claude Code and others use this shape): { \"mcpServers\": { \"animux-docs\": { \"command\": \"animux-docs\", \"args\": [\"mcp\"] } } } An agent on another computer can reach the guide through SSH ( ssh user@animux-pc animux-docs mcp ), because the web server only listens on the local machine. Recommended way for an agent to work Orient : read llms.txt , then System facts and system_info (live or installed? processor features? display?). Find the tool : search_docs or api/software.json . Each entry has the exact command and headless commands. Prefer headless commands ( blender -b … , povray … -D , ffmpeg , oiiotool ) over driving windows. They are faster, reproducible and their exit codes can be checked. See the command reference . Check the result : test the exit code, check that the output file exists and is not empty, and read the log. Health check : animux-selftest <name> (exit code 0 = fine) before blaming a program. Tell the user where the result is and how long it took. Rules for agents Do not run programs as root. Use sudo only for package installation or system settings the user asked for. Live sessions forget everything at shutdown. Before long work in a live session, tell the user; save results to a removable drive if they want ", "sec": "For AI Agents"}, {"t": "Command reference", "s": "Headless and command-line use of every Animux program, with conventions for output paths, exit codes, logs, displays and time limits.", "u": "agents/command-reference.html", "x": "Conventions Topic Rule on Animux Shell bash ; the user's Home is $HOME ; the guide's commands assume a normal user Graphical programs need the user's display: DISPLAY=:0 (X11, Xfce). Check with xdpyinfo -display :0 >/dev/null Headless programs need no display; prefer them Exit codes 0 = success for every command below. Still check the output file: some programs return 0 after writing nothing Time limits wrap long jobs: timeout 3600 blender -b … Logs redirect: cmd > job.log 2>&1 Paths with spaces quote them Parallel jobs Blender, Cycles, pbrt, LuxCore and Mitsuba each use all cores; run them one after another Python with VFX libraries animux-vfx-python (OpenUSD, OIIO, OpenEXR, OCIO, MaterialX, Mitsuba, LuxCore, NumPy) Blender's own Python blender -b --python script.py (module bpy ) Gaffer's Python gaffer env python script.py or gaffer python script.py (modules Gaffer , GafferScene , IECore ) All headless commands Program Command-line use (no window) Blender blender -b scene.blend -o //render_#### -F PNG -f 1 Blender blender -b scene.blend -a Blender blender -b --python script.py OpenSCAD openscad -o part.stl part.scad POV-Ray povray +Iscene.pov +Oscene.png +W1280 +H720 +A -D appleseed appleseed.cli scene.appleseed --output out.png LuxCoreRender animux-vfx-python /usr/share/animux/samples/luxcore/render.py out.png Mitsuba 3 mitsuba scene.xml -o out.exr Mitsuba 3 animux-vfx-python /usr/share/animux/samples/mitsuba/render.py out.exr pbrt-v4 pbrt --outfile out.exr scene.pbrt pbrt-v4 imgtool convert out.exr out.png Gaffer gaffer execute -script scene.gfr -nodes Render Gaffer gaffer python script.py OpenUSD tools (usdview, usdcat …) usdcat scene.usda -o scene.usdc OpenUSD tools (usdview, usdcat …) usdchecker scene.usda OpenUSD tools (usdview, usdcat …) usdtree scene.usda Alembic tools (abcls, abctree …) abcls -r file.abc Alembic tools (abcls, abctree …) abctree file.abc Alembic tools (abcls, abctree …) abcecho file.abc Open Shading Language (oslc, oslinfo, testshade) oslc myshader.osl Open Shading Language (oslc, oslinfo, testshade) oslinfo myshader.oso OpenEXR and OpenImageIO tools oiiotool in.exr --resize 50% -o out.png OpenEXR and OpenImageIO tools exrheader in.exr OpenEXR and OpenImageIO tools iinfo -v in.exr OpenColorIO tools ociocheck --iconfig config.ocio OpenVDB tools vdb_print -l file.vdb Animux VFX Python animux-vfx-python -c \"import pxr, OpenImageIO, PyOpenColorIO, mitsuba, pyluxcore\" Flamenco flamenco-manager Flamenco flamenco-worker -manager http://MANAGER:8080/ OpenToonz LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/animux/opentoonz/lib/opentoonz /opt/animux/opentoonz/bin/tcomposer scene.tnz -o out.png Synfig Studio synfig file.sif -t png -o frame.png Synfig Studio synfig file.sifz -t ffmpeg -o movie.mp4 GIMP gimp-console -i --batch-interpreter=python-fu-eval -b \"exec(open('script.py').read())\" --quit GIMP gimp-console -i -b '(gimp-quit 0)' Inkscape inkscape drawing.svg --export-type=png --export-width=1920 ImageMagick and G'MIC convert in.png -resize 50% out.jpg ImageMagick and G'MIC mogrify -format jpg *.png ImageMagick and G'MIC montage frame*.png -tile 4x -geometry +4+4 contact-sheet.png Avidemux avidemux-cli --load in.mp4 --save out.mkv --output-format MKV HandBrake HandBrakeCLI -i in.mov -o out.mp4 --preset \"Fast 1080p30\" FFmpeg ffmpeg -framerate 24 -i render_%04d.png -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -crf 18 movie.mp4 FFmpeg ffmpeg -i movie.mp4 -i music.wav -c:v copy -c:a aac -shortest final.mp4 Sound analysis and conversion (Sonic Visualiser, Praat, SoundConverter, SoX) sox in.wav out.flac Sound analysis and conversion (Sonic Visualiser, Praat, SoundConverter, SoX) sox in.wav -n stat Whisper animux-whisper dialogue.wav --model small --language en --output_format srt Whisper animux-whisper film.mp4 --task translate --output_format srt rembg animux-rembg i photo.jpg cutout.png rembg animux-rembg p frames/ frames-cutout/ Ollama ollama serve Ollama ollama run llama3.2 \"Write three names for a robot character\" Ollama curl http://127.0.0.1:11434/", "sec": "For AI Agents"}, {"t": "System facts", "s": "The layout of an Animux system in one page. Release, base, users, important paths, Animux packages, services, ports, boot roles and how to tell a live session from an installed one.", "u": "agents/system-facts.html", "x": "Identity Release Animux Genesis64 r16.3 , ISO animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso Base Debian 12 \"bookworm\", amd64, Linux 6.1, systemd Desktop Xfce 4.18 on X11, LightDM /etc/os-release ID=animux , ID_LIKE=debian , PRETTY_NAME=\"Animux Genesis64\" Release in machine-readable form release in /usr/share/animux/docs/api/software.json Users Situation User Password sudo Live session user (display name \"Animator\"), auto-login user without password Installed chosen in the installer chosen with password Important paths Path Contents /usr/share/animux/docs/ this guide (HTML, Markdown, JSON, llms.txt) /usr/share/animux/samples/ sample scenes for every renderer and format ~/animux-selftest/<date-time>/ self-test results, images, logs, summary.txt /opt/animux/blender/ Blender 5.2.3 ( /usr/bin/blender ), extensions in 5.2/extensions/system/ (MPFB) /opt/animux/gaffer/ Gaffer 1.7.5 with Cortex, OSL, OpenUSD, Alembic; wrappers in /usr/bin ( usdview , oslc , abcls …) /opt/animux/vfx-env/ Python 3.11 venv: Mitsuba, LuxCore, usd-core, OIIO, OpenEXR, OCIO, MaterialX ( animux-vfx-python ) /opt/animux/makehuman2/ MakeHuman II ( makehuman2 ) /opt/animux/flamenco/ Flamenco Manager and Worker /opt/animux/appleseed/ , /opt/animux/pbrt/ appleseed CLI, pbrt-v4 /opt/animux/avidemux/ , /opt/animux/cinelerra-gg/ , /opt/animux/djv/ video tools /opt/farmerjoe/ Farmerjoe /usr/lib/animux/ role selector, installer launcher, helper scripts /etc/apt/sources.list.d/animux.sources Animux repository ( Enabled: no in r16) /usr/share/keyrings/animux-archive-keyring.gpg repository signing key (fingerprint B47E D034 EDD3 3360 7502 EE4B F334 23FB B235 4B3B ) Animux packages dpkg-query -W 'animux-*' upscayl lists them: animux-archive-keyring , animux-branding , animux-menus , animux-roles , animux-docs , animux-samples , animux-farmerjoe , animux-flamenco , animux-blender , animux-blender-mpfb , animux-gaffer , animux-vfx-python , animux-makehuman2 , animux-appleseed , animux-pbrt , animux-avidemux , animux-cinelerra-gg , animux-djv , and upscayl . Services and ports Service Port Notes animux-docs.service 8765/tcp on 127.0.0.1 this guide; read-only animux-role-config.service — reads animux.role= at boot, writes /run/animux/role ssh.service 22/tcp enabled Flamenco Manager (when started) 8080/tcp web interface and API isc-dhcp-server , nfs-kernel-server — masked, except in the Render Farm Master role Boot roles The kernel parameter animux.role= selects the role; the current one is in /run/animux/role . Role Desktop Purpose workstation (default) yes normal use install yes, installer opens Calamares installation master no (display manager masked) render farm head; DHCP and NFS unmasked slave no render node Live or installed? test -d /run/live/medium && echo live || echo installed A live session keeps all changes in RAM (overlay) and loses them at shutdown. Hardware notes Blender 5, appleseed: need SSE4.2. Mitsuba, LuxCore: need SSE4.2 and AVX. OpenCL: Debian's software drivers (Mesa, PoCL) are hidden from animux-vfx-python programs because they crash LuxCore. Set ANIMUX_KEEP_ALL_OPENCL=1 to see all drivers. No NVIDIA proprietary driver on the ISO; GPU rendering in Cycles needs it ( nvidia-driver from Debian non-free). No VirtualBox/VMware guest tools on the ISO.", "sec": "For AI Agents"}, {"t": "Animux Genesis64 r16.3", "s": "OpenToonz 1.8, the studio 2D animation program, and the Maya Workflow Kit for Blender.", "u": "news/2026-10-30-r16-3-release.html", "x": "Release r16.3 ( animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso , 12 GB) adds two programs for studio animators to r16.2. New OpenToonz 1.8 , the open-source Toonz used in studios: hand-drawn and cut-out animation with the Xsheet, scan cleanup, ink and paint, and node-based effects. Menu 2D Animation → OpenToonz . Debian has no OpenToonz, so Animux builds it from its source code. A sample project is in /usr/share/animux/samples/opentoonz , and animux-selftest renders it. Maya Workflow Kit for Blender: Maya hotkeys, marking menus, shelf, Channel Box and the Maya layout, for animators trained on Autodesk Maya. It is off until you switch it on: menu Animux Tools → Blender: Maya Workflow Kit on/off . Fixed On screens of about 150 DPI (many 1080p laptops, virtual machines) OpenToonz would open at double size, with windows larger than the screen; it now follows the desktop's scale. Known limits The Animux repository is not online yet; updates for Animux packages come with the next release. The Maya Workflow Kit changes quickly upstream; Animux ships version 0.7.0, tested with Blender 5.2. Everything else as in r16.2.", "sec": "News"}, {"t": "Animux Genesis64 r16.2", "s": "Claude Code in one step from the menu, and a boot menu without syntax errors.", "u": "news/2026-10-29-r16-2-release.html", "x": "Release r16.2 ( animux-genesis64-deb_27.iso , 12 GB) is a small update of r16.1. New Claude Code in one step : the menu entry Animux Tools → Claude Code (AI agent) (or animux-mcp claude-code setup ) installs Anthropic's Claude Code with Anthropic's own installer, switches on the bridges for Blender, GIMP, Krita and Inkscape, makes a work folder and connects everything. It keeps the claude.ai connectors of your account (Gmail, Google Drive, ...) out. See AI agents . Claude Code itself is not on the ISO: it is Anthropic's program, needs a Claude account and the internet, and updates itself. Fixed The boot menu printed \"error: syntax error\" for a moment before it appeared (BIOS and UEFI). The ISO build now checks every boot menu file and stops on errors. GIMP's AI bridge hint names the right menu: Tools → MCP → Start MCP Server . Known limits The Animux repository is not online yet; updates for Animux packages come with the next release. Everything else as in r16.1.", "sec": "News"}, {"t": "Introducing the Animux Guide", "s": "A complete, illustrated guide for animators who have never used Linux, installed on every Animux system and readable by AI agents.", "u": "news/2026-10-27-animux-guide.html", "x": "Animux now comes with its own documentation: the Animux Guide , the site you are reading. For people new to Linux : from downloading the ISO and trying it in VirtualBox, to installing, the desktop, and every program, with screenshots taken on Animux itself. Tutorials that build on each other: a first render, a turntable animation, a MakeHuman character, a Synfig bouncing ball, hand-drawn animation in Pencil2D, a Krita loop, stop motion, editing in Kdenlive, command-line rendering, and a plan for a first short film. The Animux Guide in Firefox on Animux. Offline : on Animux it is under Animux Tools → Animux Guide and at http://127.0.0.1:8765/ . For AI agents : every page also exists as Markdown, with llms.txt , JSON indexes of the pages and of all software (with headless commands), a command-line tool and an MCP server. See For AI agents . The guide is published under CC BY-SA 4.0. Corrections and translations are welcome.", "sec": "News"}, {"t": "Animux Genesis64 r16 is here", "s": "Blender 5.2 LTS, Gaffer, five new renderers, MakeHuman II, a signed Animux package repository and a self-test that checks it all.", "u": "news/2026-10-26-r16-release.html", "x": "Release r16 ( animux-genesis64-deb_21.iso , 12 GB) is the largest update to Genesis64 so far. New Blender 5.2.3 LTS from blender.org replaces Debian's Blender 3.4, with the MPFB 2 character add-on switched on. Gaffer 1.7.5 , the studio lighting and look-development tool, with Cortex, OSL, OpenUSD and Alembic command-line tools. Renderers : LuxCoreRender 2.11, Mitsuba 3.9, appleseed 2.1 (command line) and pbrt-v4, next to Cycles, EEVEE and POV-Ray. MakeHuman II 2.0.1, the new stand-alone character creator. Video : Cinelerra-GG, Avidemux and the DJV review player. 2D : TupiTube and Stopmotion. Discs : K3b. Animux VFX Python ( animux-vfx-python ) with OpenUSD, OpenImageIO, OpenEXR, OpenColorIO, MaterialX, Mitsuba and LuxCore together. Sample scenes and Animux Self-Test ( Animux Tools menu). The Animux Guide (this website) is installed on every system, works offline and is readable by AI agents. Under the hood Animux's own settings (branding, menus, boot roles) are now packages from a signed Animux repository , so they can be updated with apt in future, without reinstalling. Every program from outside Debian is pinned to an exact version and checked against its published checksum when the ISO is built. Fixed : Farmerjoe could not start on earlier ISOs (missing Perl module); LuxCore crashed with the software OpenCL drivers. Tested The self-test passes 29 of 29 tests in VirtualBox (BIOS, VMSVGA). Installation to disk and the first start of the installed system were tested with UEFI. Known limits Blender 5, appleseed, Mitsuba and LuxCore need a processor from about 2011 or later (SSE4.2, AVX). The Animux repository is not online yet; updates for Animux packages come with the next release. appleseed.studio, DVDStyler and Lucille are not included. fail2ban may report an error at start-up.", "sec": "News"}, {"t": "Versions", "s": "The version of every program in Animux Genesis64 r16.3, and the package it comes from.", "u": "about/versions.html", "x": "Animux Genesis64 r16.3 ( animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso ), based on Debian 12 \"bookworm\" with Linux 6.1 and Xfce 4.18. Programs in packages named animux-* and upscayl come from the Animux repository; all others from Debian. Program Version Package Licence Alembic tools (abcls, abctree …) 1.8.3 (from Gaffer) animux-gaffer BSD-3-Clause Animux Guide 1.0.18 animux-docs CC BY-SA 4.0 Animux Sample Scenes 1.0.16.1 animux-samples GPL-3.0 Animux Self-Test 1.0.16.1 animux-samples GPL-3.0 Animux VFX Python 2026.10 animux-vfx-python various (see each library) appleseed 2.1.0-beta (command line) animux-appleseed MIT Ardour 7.3 ardour GPL-2.0 Audacity 3.2.4 audacity GPL-2.0 Audio setup (PipeWire, JACK, QjackCtl, PulseAudio Volume Control) PipeWire 0.3.65, QjackCtl 0.9.9 pipewire, pipewire-jack, qjackctl, pavucontrol MIT / GPL Avidemux 2.8.1 animux-avidemux GPL-2.0 Blender 5.2.3 LTS animux-blender GPL-3.0-or-later Catfish and Disk Usage Analyzer Debian 12 catfish, baobab GPL Cinelerra-GG 2026-08-31 animux-cinelerra-gg GPL-2.0 ComfyUI latest from GitHub at install time downloaded by animux-install-local-ai (animux-ai-tools) GPL-3.0 Cycles and EEVEE (inside Blender) Blender 5.2.3 animux-blender Apache-2.0 (Cycles), GPL (EEVEE) DJV 3.7.5 animux-djv BSD-3-Clause Farmerjoe Animux 1.0.16.1 animux-farmerjoe GPL FFmpeg 5.1 ffmpeg GPL-2.0-or-later Firefox ESR 153.5 ESR firefox-esr MPL-2.0 Flamenco 3.9.3 animux-flamenco GPL-3.0 Flatpak 1.14 flatpak LGPL-2.1 FreeCAD 0.20.2 freecad LGPL-2.0-or-later Gaffer 1.7.5.0 animux-gaffer BSD-3-Clause Geomview 1.9.5 geomview LGPL-2.1 GIMP 3.2.6 animux-gimp (r16: gimp 2.10 from Debian) GPL-3.0 GParted and Disks 1.3.1 / 43 gparted, gnome-disk-utility GPL-2.0 GrafX2 2.8 grafx2 GPL-2.0 HandBrake 1.6.1 handbrake, handbrake-cli GPL-2.0 Hydrogen 1.2.0 beta hydrogen GPL-2.0 ImageMagick and G'MIC 6.9.11 / G'MIC 2.9.4 imagemagick, gmic ImageMagick licence / CeCILL Inkscape 1.4.4 animux-inkscape (r16: inkscape 1.2 from Debian) GPL-3.0 Install DaVinci Resolve — animux-menus proprietary (free edition) K3b 22.12.3 k3b GPL-2.0 Kdenlive 22.12.3 kdenlive GPL-3.0 Krita 5.3.4.1 animux-krita (r16: krita 5.1 from Debian) GPL-3.0 LMMS 1.2.2 lmms GPL-2.0 LTX (Lightricks API) LTX-2.5 / 2.3 (cloud API) animux-mcp (animux-ltx-api) LTX API terms (paid per second) LTX-2 local (ComfyUI) nodes 1.1.0; LTX-2.5 / 2.3 models (download) downloaded by animux-install-local-ai --ltx-local (animux-ai-tools) LTX-2 Community License LuxCoreRender 2.11.2 (Python module pyluxcore) animux-vfx-python Apache-2.0 MakeHuman II 2.0.1 animux-makehuman2 AGPL-3.0 Maya Workflow Kit for Blender 0.7.0 (new in r16.3, off by default) animux-blender-maya GPL-3.0-or-later MCP servers (Blender, GIMP, Krita, Inkscape) Blender Lab 1.0.3, MCP for Blender 2.2.4, GIMP MCP 09bfb2d, Krita MCP edc458d, inkmcp 1.0.0, ollmcp 0.35.1 animux-mcp GPL-3.0-or-later (Lab), MIT (MCP for Blender, ollmcp), GPL-3.0 (GIMP MCP), CC0-1.0 (Krita MCP), AGPL-3.0 (inkmcp) MeshLab 2020.09 meshlab GPL-3.0 Mitsuba 3 3.9.1 animux-vfx-python BSD-3-Clause MKVToolNix 74.0 mkvtoolnix-gui GPL-2.0 MPFB (MakeHuman Plugin for Blender) 2.0.18 animux-blender-mpfb GPL-3.0 mtPaint 3.40 mtpaint GPL-3.0 MuseScore 3 3.2.3 musescore3 GPL-2.0 MyPaint 2.0.1 mypaint GPL-2.0 Ollama 0.40.11 (download) downloaded by animux-install-local-ai (animux-ai-tools) MIT Open Shading Language (oslc, oslinfo, testshade) from Gaffer 1.7.5 animux-gaffer BSD-3-Clause OpenColorIO tools 2.1 (Debian), 2.6 (Python) opencolorio-tools, animux-vfx-python BSD-3-Clause OpenEXR and OpenImageIO tools OpenEXR 3.1.5 / OIIO 2.4.7 (Debian), OpenEXR 3.5 / OIIO 3.2 (Python) openexr, openimageio-tools, animux-vfx-python BSD-3-Clause / Apache-2.0 OpenSCAD 2021.01 openscad GPL-2.0 OpenShot 2.6.1 openshot-qt GPL-3.0 OpenToonz 1.8.0 (built by Animux, new in r16.3) animux-opentoonz BSD-3-Clause OpenUSD tools (usdview, usdcat …) from Gaffer 1.7.5 and usd-core 26.11 animux-gaffer, animux-vfx-python TOST-1.0 (Apache-2.0 based) OpenVDB tools 10.0 libopenvdb-tools, pyth", "sec": "About"}, {"t": "Licences and credits", "s": "What free software licences mean for your work, the licence of this guide, and the people and projects behind Animux.", "u": "about/licenses.html", "x": "Your work is yours Programs like Blender, Krita or Kdenlive are licensed under the GPL or similar free licences. These licences cover the programs , not what you make with them. Your films, pictures, models and music belong to you, and you can sell them, keep them private, or share them under any licence you choose. The licences only matter if you give the programs themselves to others (for example, copying Animux for friends, which is allowed): you must pass on the same freedoms. Program licences Each program's licence is listed on its page and in Versions . The full texts are in /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright on Animux. Most common: Licence Programs (examples) In short GPL-2.0 / GPL-3.0 Blender, Krita, GIMP, Inkscape, Kdenlive, Synfig, Ardour free to use, change and share; changes must stay free AGPL-3.0 MakeHuman II, POV-Ray, Upscayl like the GPL, also for network use Apache-2.0 Cycles, pbrt-v4, LuxCoreRender free, with a patent grant BSD / MIT Gaffer, OpenEXR, OpenColorIO, Alembic, appleseed, Mitsuba, DJV free, few conditions TOST-1.0 OpenUSD Apache-2.0 based (Pixar) Assets : MakeHuman's base mesh and standard assets are CC0 (no conditions). Blender's splash-screen art belongs to its artist. The Animux wallpapers and logos belong to the Animux project. Not included for licence reasons: DaVinci Resolve (free but not open; the menu links to its download), the NVIDIA proprietary driver (installable), Lucille (no licence was ever published). This guide The Animux Guide is © the Animux project and licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 (CC BY-SA 4.0): you may copy, translate and adapt it, also for teaching, if you credit Animux and share your version under the same licence. Screenshots show the programs named on each page. Credits Animux stands on the work of thousands of volunteers: the Debian project, the Xfce team, the Blender Foundation and community, KDE (Krita, Kdenlive, K3b), the GIMP , Inkscape , Synfig , Pencil2D , MakeHuman , Ardour and Audacity teams, Image Engine (Gaffer, Cortex), Pixar (OpenUSD), the Academy Software Foundation (OpenEXR, OpenColorIO, OpenVDB, OSL, MaterialX, OpenImageIO), the authors of POV-Ray , LuxCoreRender , Mitsuba , appleseed and pbrt , and the original Animux project for animators. Thank them by using their programs, reporting bugs, and donating where you can.", "sec": "About"}, {"t": "Guide updates", "s": "What changed in each version of this guide, which version you have, and how to install a newer one without a new ISO.", "u": "about/guide-updates.html", "x": "This guide is its own small package, animux-docs . When the guide improves, only that package changes: you do not need a new ISO or a reinstall. The ISO build number ( _25 ) and the guide version ( 1.0.25 ) are separate. Which version do I have? At the bottom of every page: Animux Guide 1.0.x for Genesis64 r16.1 . In a terminal: animux-docs info (line docs_version ), or dpkg -s animux-docs | grep Version . For programs and AI agents: the version field in api/pages.json . How to get a newer guide From the Animux package repository (once it is online; see the note below): sudo apt update sudo apt install --only-upgrade animux-docs From a downloaded file ( animux-docs_<version>_all.deb ): open a terminal in the folder that holds the file and type: sudo apt install ./animux-docs_1.0.19_all.deb When sudo asks for a password, type your own login password (nothing appears while you type). In the live session no password is needed. Reload the guide in Firefox ( F5 ) afterwards. The local guide server and the animux-docs command use the new pages at once. Live session In a live session (USB stick or ISO), an updated guide lasts until you switch off, like everything else you change there. On an installed Animux it stays. Repository not online yet The Animux package repository ( https://apt.animux.org ) is prepared but not yet published, so apt cannot find updates there yet. Until it is, use the downloaded file. What changed 1.0.34 (2026-11-02) New page Renderer comparison : the seven renderers side by side (full name, maker, how they calculate light, what they run on, best at, use it for), which one when, and OSL. New page Studio formats : OpenUSD, Alembic, OpenEXR, OpenColorIO and OpenVDB, one card each: which studio made it, the problem it solves, the answer. Flamenco render farm : a card on OpenCue (Sony Pictures Imageworks and Google Cloud, 2019), planned for r17, with the sources. Used in film production : two Blender cards, Flow (Academy Award for Best Animated Feature 2025, made in Blender) and Ubisoft Animation Studio (Blender as its main 3D tool since 2019), with the sources. 1.0.33 (2026-11-02) Used in film production : a third card, Studio Ghibli (Toonz Ghibli Edition, today OpenToonz), with its source. 1.0.32 (2026-11-02) New page Used in film production : Tears of Steel (storyboards drawn in Krita) and Stuart Little 1 and 2 (textures and frame fixes in Film Gimp, a version of GIMP), with the sources. 1.0.31 (2026-11-01) New page Animux at a glance : all programs on one screen, grouped by the stages of a production (drawing, 2D, 3D, rendering, pipeline, render farm, sound, edit, AI, system), on the Debian 12 base. It is made from the software list, so it always shows the programs this guide describes. 1.0.30 (2026-10-30) The guide now describes Animux r16.3 ( animux-genesis64-deb_28.iso ): News with what is new (OpenToonz 1.8, the Maya Workflow Kit for Blender). 1.0.29 (2026-10-30) New page OpenToonz (r16.3): the studio 2D animation program, built from source by Animux. Starting it, the Startup window, rooms and the Xsheet, a first drawing, rendering with and without the window. OpenToonz is in the software list and animux-selftest opentoonz renders its sample project. FAQ and Adding software no longer send you to Flathub for it. Blender : new section Coming from Maya for the Maya Workflow Kit (r16.3, off by default; animux-blender-maya on|off|status or the menu entry Animux Tools → Blender: Maya Workflow Kit on/off ). 1.0.28 (2026-10-29) The guide now describes Animux r16.2 ( animux-genesis64-deb_27.iso ; 1.0.27 named the test build _26 , whose BIOS boot menu lost its artwork): News with what changed (Claude Code in one step from the menu, boot menu without syntax errors). 1.0.26 (2026-10-29) AI agents with Claude Code : one step instead of nine. The new menu entry Animux Tools → Claude Code (AI agent) (or animux-mcp claude-code setup , animux-mcp 1.2.0) installs Claude Code with Anthropic's installer, fixes the PATH, switc", "sec": "About"}];
